== S1
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Choose the <principal logarithm>, so $L(z)=\operatorname{Log}(1-z)$ has <branch cut> $[1,\infty)$. Near zero its <Taylor series> gives
$$
L(z)=-z-\frac{z^2}2-\frac{z^3}3+O(z^4),\qquad\frac{L(z)^2}{z^3}=\frac1z+1+O(z).
$$
Thus **zero is a simple pole of residue one**, and **one is a logarithmic branch point**. The function is <holomorphic> on $\mathbb C\setminus(\{0\}\cup[1,\infty))$ with this branch. Infinity is also a branch point in the extended-plane description; the function's decay there along the cut plane does not make it a single-valued holomorphic function through infinity.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the positive orientation shown in the source: the upper bank runs outwards and the lower bank returns towards one. On those banks the <logarithm branch> has values $\ell-i\pi$ and $\ell+i\pi$, respectively, with $\ell=\log(x-1)$. Their squared difference is $-4i\pi\ell$. The <residue theorem> therefore gives
$$
2\pi i=\lim\oint_\Gamma\frac{L(z)^2}{z^3}\,dz=-4\pi i\int_1^\infty\frac{\log(x-1)}{x^3}\,dx,
$$
and hence
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty\frac{\log(x-1)}{x^3}\,dx=-\frac12.}
$$
The large-circle contribution is $O(R^{-2}\log^2R)$ and the small circle about one contributes $O(\varepsilon|\log\varepsilon|^2)$, both tending to zero. These estimates justify replacing the closed <contour integral> by its two cut-bank <integrals>.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Take $g(z)=L(z)^3/z^3$ on the same <logarithm branch>. At zero, $L(z)^3=-z^3+O(z^4)$, so the apparent singularity is removable and the closed <contour integral> is zero. The bank difference is
$$
(\ell-i\pi)^3-(\ell+i\pi)^3=-6i\pi\ell^2+2i\pi^3.
$$
The circular contributions vanish by the previous estimates with the third power of the logarithm. Consequently
$$
0=-6i\pi\int_1^\infty\frac{\log^2(x-1)}{x^3}\,dx+2i\pi^3\int_1^\infty\frac{dx}{x^3}.
$$
The last <integral> is $1/2$, giving
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty\frac{\log^2(x-1)}{x^3}\,dx=\frac{\pi^2}{6}.}
$$
Increasing the logarithm power has converted the desired real <integral> into a jump across a <branch cut>, while removing the pole inside the contour.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

A <conformal map> preserves the magnitude and orientation of angles between intersecting smooth curves locally. For a complex <holomorphic function>, the condition at $z_0$ is $f'(z_0)\ne0$: the first-order change $\delta w=f'(z_0)\delta z$ rotates and uniformly scales every tangent vector by the same complex factor. This local statement does not require a globally one-to-one map.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

**No region maps onto the entire finite complex plane:** $e^z$ is never zero. Its image on $\mathbb C$ is $\mathbb C\setminus\{0\}$. A fundamental strip such as
$$
\boxed{\{x+iy:x\in\mathbb R,\ -\pi<y\leq\pi\}}
$$
maps bijectively onto that punctured plane, because $e^{x+iy}=e^xe^{iy}$. The half-open strip supplies one representative of each angle; it is not an open <domain>. An open strip of height $2\pi$ omits a ray as well. The missing zero is approached only in the limit $x\to-\infty$.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Writing $z=x+iy$ gives $|w|=e^x$ and $\arg w=y$. Thus the closed square maps to the annular sector
$$
\boxed{1\leq|w|\leq e^a,\qquad0\leq\arg w\leq a.}
$$
Its horizontal edges become radial segments and its vertical edges become circular arcs. Each radial tangent is perpendicular to the tangent of its adjoining circle, so the four interior angles remain $\pi/2$. Equivalently, the complex <derivative> $e^z$ is nonzero at every vertex and the map is <conformal> there.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the <complex logarithm> with $0\leq\arg w\leq\pi/2$ on the nonzero points of the first-quadrant square. Put $z=x+iy=\log|w|+i\arg w$. The square bounds $\operatorname{Re}w,\operatorname{Im}w\leq a$ give
$$
\boxed{0\leq y\leq\frac\pi2,\qquad x\leq\log a-\log\max(\cos y,\sin y).}
$$
The two edges meeting at $w=0$ map to the horizontal half-lines $y=0$ and $y=\pi/2$, extending indefinitely to the left. The other edges map to $x=\log a-\log\cos y$ for $0\leq y\leq\pi/4$ and $x=\log a-\log\sin y$ for $\pi/4\leq y\leq\pi/2$. They meet at $\log(a\sqrt2)+i\pi/4$.

**The angles are preserved at $a$, $a(1+i)$ and $ia$.** There $d\log w/dw=1/w$ is finite and nonzero. The origin is a <branch point>, has no finite logarithmic image, and supplies no fourth finite vertex at which angle preservation could be asserted.

\Image[2015-s1-conformal-square-maps.png]
{title=A square, its exponential annular sector, and its logarithmic image extending to the left}

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

For $t>0$, close the real-axis <contour> in the upper half-plane: $|e^{izt}|=e^{-t\operatorname{Im}z}$ supplies decay. The only enclosed <simple pole> is $z=i$, with <residue> $e^{-t}/(2i)$. Thus $h(t)=2\pi i\,e^{-t}/(2i)=\pi e^{-t}$.

For $t<0$, close downwards. The clockwise orientation contributes $-2\pi i$ times the <residue> $e^t/(-2i)$ at $z=-i$, giving
$$
\boxed{h(t)=\pi e^{-|t|}.}
$$
At $t=0$, direct integration of $1/(1+p^2)$ gives $\pi$, consistent with the continuous extension. The large arc tends to zero by <Jordan's lemma> for nonzero $t$, or the denominator estimate when $t=0$.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $I$ denote the requested <integral>. Symmetry and the period of $\sin^2\theta$ make the corresponding integral over $0\leq\theta\leq2\pi$ equal to $4I$. With $z=e^{i\theta}$, this becomes
$$
4I=\oint_{|z|=1}\frac{z\,dz}{i(-2z^4+5z^2-2)}.
$$
The denominator factors as $-2(z^2-1/2)(z^2-2)$. The two poles inside the <unit circle> are $z=\pm1/\sqrt2$. Each <residue> is $1/(6i)$, because the derivative of the polynomial denominator there is $6z$. The <residue theorem> gives $4I=2\pi/3$, so
$$
\boxed{I=\frac\pi6.}
$$

== S2
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For the <two-body problem>, the <centre of mass> lies a distance $aM_J/(M_\odot+M_J)=a/1048$ from the Sun. With $a=5.2\,\mathrm{AU}$,
$$
\boxed{r_\odot\simeq7.42\times10^8\,\mathrm m\simeq1.07R_\odot.}
$$
The relative circular-orbit period is $P=2\pi\sqrt{a^3/[G(M_\odot+M_J)]}\simeq11.85$ years. The Sun's orbital <speed> about that <centre of mass> is therefore
$$
\boxed{v_\odot=2\pi r_\odot/P\simeq12.5\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
This is the space-speed amplitude; the observed <radial velocity> amplitude also includes the orbital inclination.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

<Kepler's third law> is $P^2=4\pi^2a^3/[G(M_S+M_P)]$, with $a$ the semimajor axis of the relative <orbit>. For a circular edge-on <orbit>, the star's speed is $V_S=2\pi a_S/P$, and the <centre-of-mass> relation gives $a_S=aM_P/(M_S+M_P)$. Cubing the speed expression and eliminating $a$ yields
$$
\frac{M_P^3}{(M_S+M_P)^2}=\frac{PV_S^3}{2\pi G}.
$$
For $M_P\ll M_S$, this reduces to
$$
\boxed{M_P^3\simeq\frac{V_S^3PM_S^2}{2\pi G}.}
$$
Without the edge-on assumption, a factor $\sin^3i$ multiplies the numerator, explaining the minimum-mass degeneracy of the <radial velocity method>.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Converting luminosity to mass needs a <main-sequence mass-luminosity relation>. Using the near-solar approximation $L/L_\odot\simeq(M/M_\odot)^4$ gives $M_S\simeq0.946M_\odot$. Then
$$
M_P=V_S\left(\frac{PM_S^2}{2\pi G}\right)^{1/3},\qquad a=\left(\frac{GM_SP^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
For $P=11.6$ days and $V_S=10\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, these give approximately
$$
\boxed{M_P\simeq0.107M_J,\qquad a\simeq0.0985\,\mathrm{AU}.}
$$
These estimates retain the circular, edge-on and low-companion-mass assumptions. Adopting a mass-luminosity exponent $3.5$ instead changes the quoted values by about one percent; luminosity alone does not determine a unique stellar mass without such a calibration.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The <effective temperature> is defined by the <Stefan-Boltzmann law> applied to a star's total luminosity:
$$
\boxed{L=4\pi R^2\sigma_{\rm SB}T_{\rm eff}^4.}
$$
Here $R$ is the adopted photospheric radius and $\sigma_{\rm SB}$ is the <Stefan-Boltzmann constant>. This defines the temperature of a black body radiating the same total flux per unit surface area; it does not assert that every wavelength of the stellar spectrum follows a perfect <Planck spectrum>.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The <parallax> gives $d=1/0.00763\simeq131\,\mathrm{pc}$. Ignoring extinction, the <distance modulus> gives
$$
M_{\rm bol}=m_{\rm bol}-5\log_{10}(d/10\,\mathrm{pc})\simeq-6.29.
$$
The <bolometric magnitude> relation then yields $L/L_\odot=10^{0.4(4.72-M_{\rm bol})}\simeq2.53\times10^4$. With $T_\odot\simeq5772\,\mathrm K$,
$$
\boxed{\frac R{R_\odot}=\sqrt{\frac L{L_\odot}}\left(\frac{T_\odot}{3500\,\mathrm K}\right)^2\simeq4.3\times10^2.}
$$
A star this cool and luminous belongs to the **red-supergiant region** of the <Hertzsprung-Russell diagram>. Extinction and the adopted <effective temperature> are important systematic inputs; this calculation uses the supplied temperature to infer the radius.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

A first distance-independent method fits the continuum shape or a calibrated <colour index> to an atmosphere model. A black-body approximation uses <Wien's displacement law>, $\lambda_{\max}T\simeq2.90\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m\,K}$, or ratios of the <Planck spectrum> at two wavelengths. It requires a known extinction correction and sufficient treatment of molecular absorption and line blanketing; a cool supergiant need not have a black-body colour temperature equal to its <effective temperature>.

A second method fits temperature-sensitive <spectral lines> or molecular bands. <Boltzmann excitation> and the <Saha equation> explain why excitation and ionization ratios change with temperature; detailed atmosphere calculations also include surface gravity, composition and departures from <local thermodynamic equilibrium>. TiO molecular bands are particularly useful for cool stars. Line ratios remove the overall distance normalization, but their calibration remains model dependent.

The direct flux-radius method can be made distance independent as well: an independently measured angular diameter $\Theta$ gives $T_{\rm eff}=[4F_{\rm bol}/(\sigma_{\rm SB}\Theta^2)]^{1/4}$. For a large nearby supergiant with a resolved photosphere, good bolometric flux and a limb-darkened <interferometric angular diameter>, this is often the most direct determination. Its advantage depends on those measurement uncertainties and a consistent radius definition. The radius inferred in the preceding part cannot itself provide an independent temperature measurement, because the supplied temperature was already used to calculate it.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The <virial theorem> for a stationary isolated system with inverse-square gravity is
$$
\boxed{2\langle K\rangle+\langle W\rangle=0,}
$$
where $K$ is total kinetic energy in the system's <centre-of-mass frame> and $W$ is its negative gravitational potential energy. The brackets denote a suitable time or ensemble average. Surface pressure, rapid evolution or mass fluxes add terms, so equilibrium and an appropriate boundary are substantive assumptions.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For an isotropic <velocity dispersion>, $\langle v^2\rangle=3\sigma_{\rm los}^2$. To specify the order-unity structure factor, approximate the gravitating system by a uniform sphere, whose <gravitational potential energy> is $W=-3GM^2/(5R)$. The <virial theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{M_{\rm dyn}\simeq\frac{5R\sigma_{\rm los}^2}{G}\simeq9.4\times10^{14}M_\odot.}
$$
The numerical value uses $R=1\,\mathrm{Mpc}$ and $\sigma_{\rm los}=900\,\mathrm{km\,s^{-1}}$. Different mass profiles change the prefactor, rather than the scaling $R\sigma^2/G$.

For fully ionized intracluster gas, take mean particle mass $\mu m_p$ with $\mu\simeq0.6$. In the same uniform-sphere virial estimate, replace $\sigma^2$ by $k_BT/(\mu m_p)$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{M_X\simeq\frac{5Rk_BT}{G\mu m_p}\simeq3.2\times10^{15}M_\odot.}
$$
Alternatively, <hydrostatic equilibrium> gives $M(<R)=-k_BTR(G\mu m_p)^{-1}(d\log\rho_g/d\log r+d\log T/d\log r)$. An isothermal gas with density proportional to $r^{-2}$ gives $1.3\times10^{15}M_\odot$. The supplied radius and temperature do not specify the profile, so the meaningful inference is a mass of order $10^{15}M_\odot$, with the displayed model choices making the coefficient explicit. The high supplied temperature need not yield the same estimate as the galaxy dispersion in a crude single-profile calculation.

The stellar estimate is much smaller:
$$
\boxed{M_*\simeq1000(5\times10^9)(3)M_\odot=1.5\times10^{13}M_\odot.}
$$
It counts luminous stellar matter, rather than all matter in the cluster.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

**Nonbaryonic dark matter** can provide the additional gravitating mass. <Gravitational lensing> independently maps cluster mass, while the <cosmic microwave background> and <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> constrain the cosmic matter and baryon budgets. The separation of lensing mass from strongly interacting X-ray gas in merging clusters is evidence that much of the gravitating component is not that hot gas.

**Uncounted baryonic matter**, including the observed hot gas, faint stars and compact remnants, certainly adds to the stellar estimate. X-ray emission directly establishes a large gas reservoir. However, baryon-budget constraints and <microlensing> constraints on compact objects prevent arbitrarily filling the entire discrepancy with ordinary hidden objects.

**Modified gravity** could change the inference from a measured acceleration to a Newtonian mass. Such a theory must also reproduce the observed lensing and cosmological evidence; explaining some rotation curves alone is insufficient, and cluster mass discrepancies remain a demanding test. Departures from equilibrium and projection can bias individual mass estimates, but would have to explain the independent observations systematically rather than merely modify one virial prefactor.

== S3
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The one-dimensional <time-dependent Schrödinger equation> is
$$
i\hbar\partial_t\psi=\left[-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\partial_x^2+V(x)\right]\psi.
$$
The left side generates time evolution; the two terms on the right are the kinetic and potential parts of the <Hamiltonian operator>. For a time-independent potential and an <energy eigenstate>, set $\psi(x,t)=\phi(x)e^{-iEt/\hbar}$. Cancelling the time factor gives the <time-independent Schrödinger equation>, $-\hbar^2\phi''/(2m)+V\phi=E\phi$.

By the <Born rule>, $|\psi|^2dx$ is the position probability in an interval of width $dx$, with $\int|\psi|^2dx=1$ for a normalized particle. Therefore $\boxed{[\psi]=\mathrm{length}^{-1/2}}$ in one dimension. An ideal scattering plane wave instead uses flux normalization and is not itself a normalizable single-particle state on the whole line.

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

The reflection-one result describes a semi-infinite <potential step>, rather than a finite-width barrier. Define $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$ and $\kappa=\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\hbar$. For $E>0$, take
$$
\phi(x)=\begin{cases}e^{ikx}+r e^{-ikx},&x<0,\\c e^{-\kappa x},&x>0.\end{cases}
$$
The growing exponential is excluded by boundedness as $x\to\infty$. Continuity of $\phi$ and $\phi'$ at the finite step gives $1+r=c$ and $ik(1-r)=-\kappa c$, hence
$$
\boxed{r=\frac{k-i\kappa}{k+i\kappa},\qquad c=\frac{2k}{k+i\kappa},\qquad R=|r|^2=1.}
$$
The <probability current> of the decaying tail is zero; the reflected and incident currents have equal magnitude. This agrees with the classical reflection coefficient even though the spatial wave function differs from a classical trajectory.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

On the incident side, $\phi$ is the superposition of incoming and reflected waves, producing a standing-wave pattern whose travelling-wave <de Broglie wavelength> is $\lambda=2\pi/k$. On the forbidden side, it decays exponentially and remains nonzero near the boundary. A sketch should therefore show oscillations for $x<0$, a continuous function and slope at zero, and the evanescent tail for $x>0$ beside the potential step.

The quantum distinction is **finite probability density inside the classically forbidden region**, despite complete reflection. There is no transmitted flux at arbitrarily large positive $x$ for this semi-infinite step. A finite-width barrier would allow <quantum tunnelling>, a different boundary-value problem.

\Image[2015-s3-potential-step.png]
{title=A reflected wave joins a decaying tail inside a semi-infinite potential step}

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

The wave-function amplitude decays as $e^{-\kappa x}$, so its e-folding penetration depth is
$$
\boxed{\Delta x=\kappa^{-1}=\frac\hbar{\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}}.}
$$
The <probability density> decays as $e^{-2\kappa x}$ and has the smaller e-folding depth $1/(2\kappa)$. Specifying which quantity decays avoids a factor-of-two ambiguity in the term penetration depth.

==== d
{scope}

===== Solution

A stationary evanescent wave does not assign a classical trajectory or a real local speed inside the forbidden region. The <dwell time in a semi-infinite potential step> measures probability stored in $x>0$ divided by incident <probability current>. For incident amplitude one,
$$
\tau_D=\frac{\int_0^\infty|c|^2e^{-2\kappa x}dx}{\hbar k/m}
=\boxed{\frac{2mk}{\hbar\kappa(k^2+\kappa^2)}}.
$$
This gives a time in terms of $m,E,V_0$ through the definitions of $k,\kappa$. A simpler dimensional estimate is the penetration length divided by the incident speed, $\Delta x/v_{\rm in}=m/(\hbar k\kappa)=\hbar/[2\sqrt{E(V_0-E)}]$. The dwell time equals $(2E/V_0)$ times that estimate. Different operational timing definitions need not agree; treating the imaginary under-barrier momentum as a real particle velocity would not justify a unique traversal time.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Using $m\simeq4m_p$ and $T=15\,\mathrm{meV}$, the nonrelativistic <de Broglie wavelength> and speed are
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac h{\sqrt{2mT}}\simeq1.17\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m,\qquad v\simeq8.48\times10^2\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
For the square interferometer, the enclosed area projected into a vertical plane is $a^2\sin\theta$. The <gravitational phase shift in matter-wave interferometry> is $\Delta\varphi=mg a^2\sin\theta/(\hbar v)$ to leading order in the small gravitational energy change. Thus rotation from horizontal to vertical changes the fringe count by
$$
\boxed{N=\frac{mg a^2}{hv}\simeq1.17\times10^3,\qquad N_{\rm Moon}\simeq1.95\times10^2.}
$$
One way to see the phase is that the horizontal arms differ in gravitational potential energy by $mg a\sin\theta$ for a flight time $a/v$. Which-path detection entangles the atom with distinguishable detector states. If those states are orthogonal and the path record is ignored, their coherence is lost and the interference fringes disappear. This is a loss of interference visibility, not a change in the atom's <de Broglie wavelength>.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For the macroscopic mass,
$$
\boxed{\lambda=\frac h{mv}=\frac{6.626\times10^{-34}}{2(33)}\simeq1.00\times10^{-35}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The ideal fixed-momentum expression tends to infinity as $v\to0$. An everyday localized object is not a perfectly coherent momentum eigenstate, however: its thermal and environmental <momentum uncertainty> remains nonzero, and <decoherence> rapidly suppresses spatially separated coherent alternatives. Merely setting the mean velocity to zero does not prepare an object with a macroscopic <coherence length>.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For a uniformly illuminated <single slit> of width $w$, the far-field amplitude is proportional to $\sin u/u$, with $u=\pi w\sin\vartheta/\lambda$. Its first zeros occur at $\sin\vartheta=\pm\lambda/w$. If the quoted peak width is the full width between those zeros, the small-angle geometry gives
$$
\Delta y\simeq\frac{2L\lambda}{w},\qquad\boxed{w\simeq\frac{2L\lambda}{\Delta y}\simeq2.3\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The <Fraunhofer diffraction> approximation is appropriate since $w^2/(\lambda L)\ll1$. If the supplied width instead meant a half-width or full width at half maximum, its numerical conversion factor would differ; the displayed answer states the first-zero convention explicitly.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The normalized linear <polarization state> is $|\theta\rangle=\cos\theta|H\rangle+\sin\theta|V\rangle$. A transmitted-photon count implements the <projector> $|\theta\rangle\langle\theta|$. The <Born rule> gives
$$
\boxed{p(\theta)=|\langle\theta|H\rangle|^2=\cos^2\theta,}
$$
the single-photon form of <Malus's law>, assuming ideal polarizers and detection.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For the <product state> $|H\rangle_A|H\rangle_B$, the two projection amplitudes multiply. Therefore
$$
\boxed{p(\theta_A,\theta_B)=\cos^2\theta_A\cos^2\theta_B.}
$$
The independent factors arise from this product input; they cannot be assumed for a general correlated pair state.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

The classical mixture has <density matrix> $\rho=\tfrac12|HH\rangle\langle HH|+\tfrac12|VV\rangle\langle VV|$. Average the probabilities of its two alternatives:
$$
\boxed{p(A,B)=\frac12[\cos^2\theta_A\cos^2\theta_B+\sin^2\theta_A\sin^2\theta_B].}
$$
Alice's single-detector transmission probability is $1/2$ at every angle. Dividing by it gives
$$
\boxed{p(B\mid A,\theta_A=45^\circ)=\frac12,\qquad p(B\mid A,\theta_A=0)=\cos^2\theta_B.}
$$
At $45^\circ$, Alice's click does not distinguish the two classical alternatives. At zero, it selects the horizontally polarized alternative.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The normalized <Bell state>
$$
\boxed{|\Phi^+\rangle=\frac{|HH\rangle+|VV\rangle}{\sqrt2}}
$$
has equal outcomes for any common real linear-polarization basis. In the stated rotated basis it is $(|\theta^{(+)}\theta^{(+)}\rangle+|\theta^{(-)}\theta^{(-)}\rangle)/\sqrt2$: the cross terms cancel when the inverse rotation is substituted.

Thus the coincidence probability for the transmitted ports is $p(A,B)=\tfrac12\cos^2(\theta_A-\theta_B)$. At equal angles it is **$1/2$ per emitted pair**, while Bob transmits with probability **one conditional on Alice's transmission**. Both photons are blocked for the other half of ideal pairs. The phrase always in coincidence must therefore mean matching outcomes or conditional transmission, rather than unit unconditional transmission through both polarizers.

The classical mixture has no coherence between $HH$ and $VV$; it gives equal-angle coincidence probability $1/4$ at $45^\circ$, rather than $1/2$. The coherent <quantum superposition> maintains correlation in this rotated basis. Bob's unconditional transmission remains $1/2$, so the conditional correlation does not permit signalling by Alice's polarizer choice.

\Image[2015-s3-polarization-coincidences.png]
{title=Classical and entangled photon-pair coincidence probabilities in rotated linear-polarization bases}

== S7
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Hamilton's principle> makes the <action> $S[q]=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}L(q,\dot q,t)dt$ stationary under variations vanishing at the endpoints. It need not be a strict minimum. Varying the action and integrating the velocity variation by parts gives
$$
\delta S=\left[\sum_i\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\delta q_i\right]_{t_1}^{t_2}+\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\sum_i\left[\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}-\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\right]\delta q_i\,dt.
$$
The endpoint term vanishes. Since the interior variations are arbitrary, the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> yields
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}=0.}
$$

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Write $u=\pi x^2/l_0^2$ and assume $V_0>0$. The derivative of the potential is $V'(x)=-2\pi V_0x\sin u/l_0^2$. Its equilibrium positions are zero and $x=\pm l_0\sqrt n$ for positive integers $n$. At a nonzero equilibrium,
$$
V''=-\frac{4\pi^2V_0n}{l_0^2}(-1)^n.
$$
Thus the stable equilibria and their small-oscillation <normal mode frequencies> are
$$
\boxed{x_n=\pm l_0\sqrt n,\quad\omega_n=\frac{2\pi}{l_0}\sqrt{\frac{nV_0}{m}},\quad n=1,3,5,\ldots.}
$$
Even positive $n$ give unstable maxima. At zero the quadratic term vanishes but $V(x)=V_0-V_0\pi^2x^4/(2l_0^4)+\cdots$, so the equilibrium is still unstable; a zero linearized frequency does not prove stability. If the unspecified sign of $V_0$ were chosen negative, even-$n$ positions would be stable instead, together with a degenerate quartic minimum at zero whose frequency tends to zero with oscillation amplitude.

\Image[2015-s7-quadratic-phase-potential.png]
{title=Stable minima of the cosine-of-square potential and their small-oscillation frequencies}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

For an infinitesimal continuous symmetry $\delta q_i=\varepsilon\xi_i(q,t)$, allow the <Lagrangian> to change by a total derivative, $\delta L=\varepsilon\,dF/dt$. Direct variation gives
$$
\frac{\delta L}{\varepsilon}=\sum_i\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}-\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}\right)\xi_i+\frac d{dt}\sum_i p_i\xi_i.
$$
On a solution of the <Euler-Lagrange equations>, the first term vanishes. This proves <Noether's theorem> in the fixed-time form:
$$
\boxed{Q=\sum_i p_i\xi_i-F,\qquad\dot Q=0.}
$$
Spatial translations yield total <momentum>, and rotations yield <angular momentum>. Time translations yield <energy> when the Lagrangian has no explicit time dependence; this follows either from the extended time-transformation form of the theorem or by differentiating $H=\sum_ip_i\dot q_i-L$ to obtain $\dot H=-\partial L/\partial t$.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The central pair potential is invariant under simultaneous spatial translations, simultaneous rotations and time translations. The corresponding time-independent <Noether charges> are total <momentum> $P=p_1+p_2$, total <angular momentum> about a fixed origin, and total <energy> $H$.

In one dimension this supplies **$P$ and $H$: two charges**. In two dimensions it supplies **$P_x,P_y,L_{xy},H$: four charges**. In three dimensions it supplies **three momentum components, three angular-momentum components and $H$: seven charges**. For generic states these listed charges are independent in those dimensions. Galilean boosts can also be expressed by explicitly time-dependent conserved combinations, but are not part of the requested translation-rotation-energy count.

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

In $D$ dimensions the antisymmetric <angular momentum tensor> has components
$$
L_{ab}=\sum_{s=1}^2(q_{s,a}p_{s,b}-q_{s,b}p_{s,a}),\qquad a<b.
$$
There are $D(D-1)/2$ independent rotation generators. Together with $D$ translation generators and one time-translation generator, the standard <Noether charge> enumeration is therefore
$$
\boxed{1+D+\frac{D(D-1)}2=1+\frac{D(D+1)}2.}
$$
The word independent here counts symmetry generators and tensor components; it needs qualification if it is meant as functional independence of all charges for only two particles in arbitrarily high dimension. In <centre-of-mass coordinates>, $L=R\wedge P+r\wedge p$. Consequently $P\wedge L\wedge L=0$, a nontrivial identity from dimension five onwards, since $(r\wedge p)\wedge(r\wedge p)=0$. Thus the displayed number is the symmetry-charge count; it is not an assertion that these functions have unrestricted values for a two-particle realization in every $D$.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The <canonical momentum> is $p_i=\partial L/\partial\dot q_i$. If the velocity-momentum relation can be locally inverted, the <Legendre transform> defines $H(q,p,t)=\sum_ip_i\dot q_i-L$, with the velocities expressed in terms of $q,p,t$. Its differential is
$$
dH=\sum_i\dot q_i\,dp_i-\sum_i\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_i}dq_i-\frac{\partial L}{\partial t}dt.
$$
The <Euler-Lagrange equations> then yield <Hamilton's equations>,
$$
\boxed{\dot q_i=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i},\qquad\dot p_i=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i}.}
$$
A singular velocity Hessian instead requires a constrained-Hamiltonian treatment.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The <canonical momentum> is $p=\gamma m\dot r$, where $\gamma=(1-\dot r^2/c^2)^{-1/2}$. Eliminating velocity gives
$$
\boxed{H=\gamma mc^2=\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2p^2}.}
$$
Its <Hamilton equations> are $\dot r=c^2p/H$ and $\dot p=0$. In the limit $|p|\ll mc$, the <Taylor expansion> is $H=mc^2+p^2/(2m)+O(p^4/(m^3c^2))$, and $\dot r\simeq p/m$. The additive rest energy does not change the classical free-particle equations.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Minimal electromagnetic coupling changes the <canonical momentum> to $p=\gamma m\dot r+qA$. The <mechanical momentum> is $\pi=p-qA$. The <Legendre transform> yields
$$
\boxed{H(r,p,t)=\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2[p-qA(r,t)]^2}+q\phi(r,t).}
$$
The cancellation of $q\dot r\cdot A$ in the Legendre transform does not remove magnetic effects: $A$ remains in the relation between canonical and mechanical momenta. The expression is the relativistic kinetic-plus-rest energy with an electrostatic potential-energy term, expressed in canonical variables.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $\mathcal E=\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2\pi^2}$. <Hamilton's equations> give
$$
\boxed{\dot r_i=\frac{c^2\pi_i}{\mathcal E},\qquad\dot p_i=q\sum_j\dot r_j\partial_iA_j-q\partial_i\phi.}
$$
Subtracting $q\,dA_i/dt$ from the second equation gives the familiar <Lorentz force>, $\dot\pi=q(E+\dot r\times B)$, with $E=-\nabla\phi-\partial_tA$ and $B=\nabla\times A$. This also checks the signs of both potential terms.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The canonical <Poisson bracket> is
$$
\{f,g\}=\sum_k\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial q_k}\frac{\partial g}{\partial p_k}-\frac{\partial f}{\partial p_k}\frac{\partial g}{\partial q_k}\right).
$$
The coordinate derivatives are <Kronecker deltas>, so direct substitution gives
$$
\boxed{\{q_i,q_j\}=0,\qquad\{p_i,p_j\}=0,\qquad\{q_i,p_j\}=\delta_{ij}.}
$$

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For a phase-space function with no explicit time dependence, the <chain rule> and <Hamilton's equations> give
$$
\frac{df}{dt}=\sum_i(\partial_{q_i}f\,\dot q_i+\partial_{p_i}f\,\dot p_i)=\sum_i(\partial_{q_i}f\,\partial_{p_i}H-\partial_{p_i}f\,\partial_{q_i}H)=\boxed{\{f,H\}}.
$$
An explicitly time-dependent function would instead satisfy $df/dt=\partial_tf+\{f,H\}$.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

The <Jacobi identity> is $\{f,\{g,h\}\}+\{g,\{h,f\}\}+\{h,\{f,g\}\}=0$. If $f,g$ have no explicit time dependence and are conserved, $\{f,H\}=\{g,H\}=0$. Taking $h=H$ in that identity yields $\{H,\{f,g\}\}=0$, or
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\{f,g\}=\{\{f,g\},H\}=0.}
$$
Thus the <Poisson bracket> of the two conserved functions is another conserved function; it need not be independent of the original ones.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

For $L_i=\epsilon_{ijk}q_jp_k$, the canonical <Poisson brackets> give $\{L_i,L_j\}=\epsilon_{ijk}L_k$. Consequently projections onto fixed axes $n,m$ obey
$$
\{n\cdot L,m\cdot L\}=(n\times m)\cdot L.
$$
If the two original projections are conserved and the axes are linearly independent, the preceding <Jacobi identity> argument makes the projection along $n\times m$ conserved as well. The vectors $n,m,n\times m$ form a <basis> of three-dimensional space, so every fixed-axis projection is a <linear combination> of these conserved quantities. **Angular momentum is therefore conserved along every axis.**

==== 5
{scope}

===== Solution

For the standard meaning of a <canonical transformation>, the new coordinates must retain Hamiltonian form for every Hamiltonian, with the same physical function expressed in the new coordinates. Let $z=(q,p)$, $Z=F(z)=(Q,P)$, $S=DF$ and $J=\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\-I&0\end{pmatrix}$. With $K(Z)=H(F^{-1}(Z))$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\dot Z=S J S^T\nabla_ZK.
$$
Agreement with $\dot Z=J\nabla_ZK$ for arbitrary $K$ forces $SJS^T=J$. Its matrix entries are precisely the <Poisson brackets> of the new coordinates, so
$$
\boxed{\{Q_i,Q_j\}=0,\qquad\{P_i,P_j\}=0,\qquad\{Q_i,P_j\}=\delta_{ij}.}
$$
The qualification for every Hamiltonian matters. <Preserving one Hamiltonian flow does not imply canonicality>. For example, with two coordinate pairs and $H=(q_1^2+p_1^2)/2$, the invertible change $Q_1=q_1$, $P_1=p_1$, $Q_2=2q_2$, $P_2=p_2$ preserves the equations and the same physical Hamiltonian, but $\{Q_2,P_2\}=2$. Thus a definition requiring only one chosen Hamiltonian flow is too weak to prove the claimed bracket identities. The universal definition supplies the missing condition.

== S10
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For a narrow, well-collimated <photon> beam, scattered <photons> are rejected and the uncollided <intensity> obeys the <Beer-Lambert law>, $I(x)=I(0)e^{-\mu x}$. With water <mass density> $\rho\simeq1\,\mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}$, the <linear attenuation coefficient> is $\mu=\rho(\mu/\rho)$. The <half-value layer> is therefore
$$
\boxed{x_{1/2}=\frac{\ln2}{\mu},\qquad x_{1/2}(140\,\mathrm{keV})=4.51\,\mathrm{cm},\qquad x_{1/2}(511\,\mathrm{keV})=7.22\,\mathrm{cm}.}
$$
Thus the <annihilation photons> associated with the fluorine-18 source require about $1.60$ times as much water for the same attenuation as the technetium-99m <photons>. The $511\,\mathrm{keV}$ here is the <annihilation photon> energy, not the variable kinetic energy of an emitted <positron>. A broad-beam measurement admitting scattered <photons> would require a buildup correction and would not have this simple transmission interpretation.<Tables of X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients and Mass Energy-Absorption Coefficients by John H. Hubbell and Stephen M. Seltzer>[§2, equations 1–3: uncollided transmission and mass thickness]{ref}

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For <photons>, the total <mass attenuation coefficient> sums contributions from <photoelectric absorption>, <Compton scattering>, coherent <Rayleigh scattering>, and <pair production> in nuclear or electronic fields when above their thresholds. At sufficiently high <photon energies>, <photonuclear reactions> can also contribute. Attenuation counts removal from the incident narrow beam, including scattering without complete local deposition of the original energy.

In water at both specified energies, <Compton scattering> dominates. At $140\,\mathrm{keV}$, <photoelectric absorption> and coherent <Rayleigh scattering> make smaller contributions; at $511\,\mathrm{keV}$ these are still less important. Nuclear-field <pair production> is impossible below $2m_ec^2=1.022\,\mathrm{MeV}$, so it occurs in neither example. The positrons used to produce the fluorine-18 annihilation radiation do not change that threshold for interactions of its $511\,\mathrm{keV}$ <photons> in water.

Strictly, $\mu$ is a <linear attenuation coefficient>, whereas $\mu/\rho$ is a <mass attenuation coefficient>; the source's wording blurs these units. The <mass energy-absorption coefficient> $\mu_{\rm en}/\rho$ weights the fraction of incident <photon energy> transferred to charged particles and retained after radiative losses, rather than simply counting all removals. Coherent scattering contributes attenuation with negligible energy transfer. A <photon> deflected by <Compton scattering> can carry much of the original energy away. One often writes $\mu_{\rm en}=(1-g)\mu_{\rm tr}$, with $g$ the fraction of secondary charged-particle kinetic energy lost radiatively. Under <charged-particle equilibrium>, multiplying <photon energy fluence> by $\mu_{\rm en}/\rho$ gives the <absorbed dose>; that equilibrium condition matters near interfaces and in buildup regions.<Tables of X-Ray Mass Attenuation Coefficients and Mass Energy-Absorption Coefficients by John H. Hubbell and Stephen M. Seltzer>[§§2–3, definitions of total attenuation, energy transfer and energy absorption]{ref}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

A <CT number> is a dimensionless relative attenuation measure, conventionally $H=1000(\mu_{\rm CT}-\mu_{{\rm water},\rm CT})/\mu_{{\rm water},\rm CT}$. The <X-ray> spectrum used in <computed tomography> has an effective energy far below $511\,\mathrm{keV}$ and is polychromatic. Its <photoelectric absorption> contribution depends strongly on composition, whereas water and soft-tissue attenuation at the <positron emission tomography> energy is mainly <Compton scattering>. Thus a <CT number> is neither a $511\,\mathrm{keV}$ <linear attenuation coefficient> nor an energy-independent <mass attenuation coefficient>.

The usual conversion calibrates a piecewise linear or bilinear relation between <CT numbers> and $\mu(511\,\mathrm{keV})$. The air–water branch models predominantly density differences in lung and soft tissue; a separate water–bone branch accounts for the different energy dependence of mineralized tissue. The calibration depends on the <computed tomography> spectrum. After geometric registration, the resulting <linear attenuation coefficient> map is forward projected along each <coincidence line of response> to obtain $\exp(\int\mu_{511}\,ds)$. If a mass coefficient is stored, its conversion to a linear coefficient must also include <mass density>. Unusual high-atomic-number materials can violate the simple tissue calibration, so the conversion is a physical material model, not a universal arithmetic rescaling.<CT-based PET attenuation correction study (1998)>[original study: segmentation, scaling and hybrid conversion from CT energies to 511 keV]{ref}<CT-to-PET bone attenuation calibration study (2012)>[Materials and Methods: piecewise conversion and air–water versus water–bone mixture branches]{ref}

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

In the ideal unscattered coincidence model, let a <positron annihilation> occur at position $s$ on a <coincidence line of response> connecting detectors $A$ and $B$. Its two oppositely directed <photons> must both survive. Their independent attenuation factors multiply:
$$
P_{\rm coinc}(s)=\exp\left[-\int_A^s\mu(\ell)\,d\ell\right]\exp\left[-\int_s^B\mu(\ell)\,d\ell\right]=\boxed{\exp\left[-\int_A^B\mu(\ell)\,d\ell\right].}
$$
The source position cancels because the complementary path integrals span the whole chord. In a uniform slab of chord length $L$, the same argument is $e^{-\mu s}e^{-\mu(L-s)}=e^{-\mu L}$. Therefore attenuation correction requires the full <linear attenuation coefficient> map along the chord, but not the depth of the particular annihilation along that chord. This argument addresses attenuation of true <PET coincidences>; scatter, random coincidences and detector efficiency need their own corrections.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

For a megavoltage <X-ray> beam, the surface <absorbed dose> is below the subsurface maximum. <Photons> generate energetic secondary <electrons>, mainly through <Compton scattering>; the downstream electron fluence initially builds up until approximate <charged-particle equilibrium> is established. Beyond this buildup region, decreasing primary <photon fluence>, scattering and geometric spreading make the <depth-dose curve> fall gradually. A penetrating photon beam has a substantial exit dose.

A nearly monoenergetic <proton> beam deposits a moderate entrance dose, then a sharp <Bragg peak> near the end of its range. As nonrelativistic <protons> slow, their electronic <stopping power> rises approximately as $1/v^2$ over the relevant part of the track. Once the protons stop, the dose falls steeply. Range straggling and energy spread broaden the peak; nuclear interactions add a smaller nonideal tail, so an exact hard cutoff is an idealization. Superposing appropriate incident energies produces a <spread-out Bragg peak> covering an extended target.<Proton depth-dose proposal (1946)>[pp. 487–489, increasing specific ionization near the end of range]{ref}

The label MV for an <X-ray> treatment beam specifies the nominal accelerating potential of the electron accelerator. Its <bremsstrahlung> beam is a broad <photon energy> spectrum with an endpoint of order $eV$, not a monoenergetic beam whose individual photons all carry the quoted numerical energy. <Proton therapy> beam energies can instead be specified directly in MeV per incident proton.

\Image[2015-s10-depth-dose-and-passive-nozzle.png]
{title=Qualitative photon and proton depth-dose profiles and functions of passive proton beam modifiers}

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

A passively scattered <proton therapy> beam uses distinct devices to control lateral spread, range and distal shape. The lower panel of the figure gives a functional schematic; the exact ordering varies among beamlines. An energy selector or range shifter sets the maximum range. A stepped range-modulation wheel or ridge filter gives a weighted collection of proton energies, forming the <spread-out Bragg peak>. One or more scattering foils broaden the narrow beam; a double-scattering system can improve lateral uniformity. <Ionization chambers> monitor delivered fluence and position. A patient-specific aperture or collimator blocks unwanted lateral regions. A shaped range compensator makes the remaining range vary across the field, matching the distal target boundary. Their effects must be combined: lateral collimation alone cannot flatten the depth distribution, and an energy modulator alone cannot set the lateral contour.<Passive proton double-scattering study (1977)>[original experiments and design: blocking the central peak followed by rescattering to flatten a large field]{ref}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the <linear-quadratic radiobiology model>, independent fractions with complete inter-fraction repair, and no tumour repopulation. For $N$ fractions of dose $d$, the logarithmic cell kill is $N(\alpha d+\beta d^2)$. Dividing by $\alpha$ defines the <biologically effective dose>,
$$
\operatorname{BED}=Nd\left(1+\frac d{\alpha/\beta}\right).
$$
For the tumour, the original schedule gives $26(2)(1+2/10)=\boxed{62.4\,\mathrm{Gy}_{10}}$. A $3\,\mathrm{Gy}$ fraction contributes $3(1+3/10)=3.9\,\mathrm{Gy}_{10}$, so precisely $62.4/3.9=\boxed{16}$ such fractions give the same tumour <biologically effective dose>. The physical total dose changes from $52$ to $48\,\mathrm{Gy}$.

For the specified normal-tissue $\alpha/\beta=3\,\mathrm{Gy}$, the original and altered schedules give
$$
\operatorname{BED}_{\rm old}=52\left(1+\frac23\right)=86.7\,\mathrm{Gy}_3,\qquad \operatorname{BED}_{\rm new}=48\left(1+\frac33\right)=\boxed{96.0\,\mathrm{Gy}_3}.
$$
Thus the normal-tissue model predicts an increase of $9.33\,\mathrm{Gy}_3$, or about $10.8\%$, despite the smaller total physical dose. The smaller normal-tissue $\alpha/\beta$ makes increasing fraction size relatively more costly. These are comparisons within the source's hypothetical radiobiological model and equal-dose geometry, not individualized treatment prescriptions.<Linear-quadratic fractionation study (1989)>[BED formulation and dependence on fraction size and alpha/beta]{ref}

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Take room temperature as $T=300\,\mathrm K$. With the supplied <gyromagnetic ratio>, <reduced Planck constant> and <Boltzmann constant>,
$$
\Delta E=(1.054\times10^{-34})(2.675\times10^8)(1.5)=4.23\times10^{-26}\,\mathrm J,
$$
so
$$
\boxed{\frac{\Delta E}{k_{\rm B}T}=\frac{4.23\times10^{-26}}{(1.380\times10^{-23})(300)}=1.02\times10^{-5}.}
$$
At $293\,\mathrm K$ the ratio is $1.05\times10^{-5}$; the room-temperature convention makes only a small difference. The corresponding fractional <nuclear spin polarization> has magnitude about five parts per million. The source's negative sign convention assigns the label up to the higher-energy state; it should not be confused with the lower-energy magnetic moment aligned with a positive proton <magnetic field>.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Write $x=\Delta E/(k_{\rm B}T)$ and $r=N_\uparrow/N_\downarrow=e^{-x}$. Since $N=N_\uparrow+N_\downarrow$, eliminate the individual populations:
$$
\frac{N_\uparrow-N_\downarrow}{N}=\frac{r-1}{r+1}=\frac{e^{-x}-1}{e^{-x}+1}=-\tanh\frac x2.
$$
The <Taylor expansion> $e^{-x}=1-x+O(x^2)$ gives the same result, and the odd expansion of the <hyperbolic tangent> yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{N_\uparrow-N_\downarrow}{N}=-\frac x2+O(x^3)\simeq-\frac{\hbar\gamma B}{2k_{\rm B}T}.}
$$
Equivalently, $N_\uparrow\simeq(N/2)(1-x/2)$ and $N_\downarrow\simeq(N/2)(1+x/2)$. Their sum is still $N$. The negative sign follows directly from the stated higher-energy up-state population, and does not mean a negative magnitude of the equilibrium magnetization.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

There are three physical controls: alter the energy splitting, alter the temperature, or drive the spin populations away from thermal equilibrium.

Increasing the static <magnetic flux density> increases $\Delta E=\hbar\gamma B$, and hence the equilibrium <nuclear spin polarization> approximately in proportion to $B/T$. High-field <magnetic resonance imaging> exploits this for greater available magnetization and potentially finer spatial or temporal resolution. The disadvantages include more demanding magnets, stronger susceptibility-related distortions, and harder <radio-frequency> field uniformity and power management. A direct experimental comparison of head imaging at 7 T and 4 T measured both the signal gain and the RF penalties.<High-field MRI comparison (2001)>[head-imaging measurements of signal-to-noise, RF power and field homogeneity]{ref}

Lowering the *spin sample's* temperature increases its equilibrium polarization at fixed $B$. Cryogenic preparation of an imaging agent can exploit this, often assisted by <dynamic nuclear polarization>, followed by rapid dissolution and transfer for <hyperpolarized magnetic resonance imaging>. This offers a much larger signal from dilute nuclei than room-temperature equilibrium, but requires preparation hardware, rapid transfer and acquisition before <spin-lattice relaxation> erases the polarization. Cooling an RF receiver reduces electronic noise, but does not itself change the nuclei's population ratio; nor does this argument propose cooling living tissue to cryogenic temperatures. The demonstrated dissolution method combines low temperature, magnetic field and microwave-assisted polarization transfer, so its gain must not be attributed to cooling alone.<Dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization demonstration (2003)>[solid-state polarization and rapid dissolution preserving enhanced nuclear polarization]{ref}

Driving transitions with a resonant <radio-frequency> field changes the longitudinal populations without changing $B$ or the bath temperature. A $\pi$ pulse interchanges the populations; saturation equalizes them. <Inversion recovery> then measures or exploits their return toward equilibrium, $M_z(t)=M_0(1-2e^{-t/T_1})$ after an ideal inversion, providing tissue contrast and signal nulling. Advantages are selective contrast and control within an ordinary scanner; costs include reduced signal, extra acquisition time, RF energy deposition and sensitivity to pulse errors. Such RF pulses alone cannot create a polarization magnitude greater than the available equilibrium value. Genuine nonthermal enhancement instead transfers polarization from another spin reservoir, for example electron spins in <dynamic nuclear polarization> or optically polarized noble gases in lung imaging; its transient and preparation constraints differ from ordinary RF inversion.<Dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization demonstration (2003)>[introductory account distinguishing optical pumping, spin-order transfer and DNP from thermal polarization]{ref}

== S12
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The <protein primary structure> is the ordered <protein sequence> along a <polypeptide>, from its <N-terminus> to its <C-terminus>. In cells, information in a <DNA> coding sequence is copied into <messenger RNA> by <transcription>. During <translation>, the <ribosome> reads successive three-base <codons>; <transfer RNA> molecules match these by their anticodons and supply the corresponding <amino acids>. The <genetic code> is degenerate: different codons can encode the same amino acid. A start establishes the reading frame and a stop terminates translation. <Peptide bonds> link the amino acids into the backbone, leaving chemically different side chains. Thus the sequence specifies the order of side-chain interactions, rather than a literal coordinate list for the folded molecule.

<Protein secondary structure> describes local backbone arrangements. An <alpha helix> is stabilized by a regular pattern of backbone hydrogen bonds, typically between the carbonyl of residue $i$ and the amide of residue $i+4$. <Beta sheets> assemble extended strands through inter-strand hydrogen bonding, with parallel or antiparallel strand orientations. Turns and loops connect these elements; not every region belongs to a helix or sheet. The <peptide bond> is approximately planar, while backbone torsion angles provide conformational freedom subject to steric restrictions.

<Protein tertiary structure> is the complete three-dimensional organization of one chain, often arranged into <protein domains>. <Protein quaternary structure> describes the organization of multiple chains in an assembly. Soluble globular proteins often bury nonpolar side chains and expose polar ones; membrane proteins can instead expose hydrophobic surfaces to the surrounding lipid. Other stabilizing interactions include <hydrogen bonds>, electrostatic interactions, <van der Waals forces> and sometimes covalent <disulfide bonds>. These competing contributions define active sites, binding pockets and mechanically useful structures; folded proteins are dynamic ensembles, not perfectly rigid objects.

The connection from sequence to structure is thermodynamic and kinetic. Folding usually decreases the chain's <conformational entropy>, but favorable interactions and changes in solvent <entropy>, including the <hydrophobic effect>, can lower the total <Gibbs free energy>. Under suitable conditions the native ensemble is favored over unfolded alternatives. A change in sequence can therefore change the balance of accessible conformations and alter folding or function. Reversible refolding of a denatured protein in an appropriate environment demonstrates that the sequence can contain the information needed for its native structure.<Protein-chain folding principles (1973)>[thermodynamic hypothesis and refolding experiments, pp. 223–225]{ref}

The environment is part of the specification: temperature, pH, ionic conditions, ligands and post-translational modifications matter. <Molecular chaperones> can prevent aggregation and help chains escape kinetic traps without providing a residue-by-residue structural template. Some sequences are <intrinsically disordered proteins> or disordered regions, and some require partners for a stable fold. Thus primary structure strongly constrains structure, but does not guarantee one unique rigid shape independent of conditions.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Let the interface be $y=0$, with glass at $y<0$ and water at $y>0$, and choose $x$ parallel to the incident beam's tangential component. Define $k=\omega/c$ as the vacuum <wavenumber>, with glass and water <refractive indices> $n_g$ and $n_w$. Continuity of <phase> along the interface fixes $k_x=kn_g\sin\theta$. In water, the <dispersion relation> is $k_x^2+k_y^2=k^2n_w^2$, so above the <critical angle>,
$$
k_y^2=-k^2(n_g^2\sin^2\theta-n_w^2),\qquad k_y=i\kappa,\qquad \kappa=k\sqrt{n_g^2\sin^2\theta-n_w^2}>0.
$$
Choose the positive imaginary part because a field growing exponentially into the water would be unphysical. The <evanescent field in total internal reflection> is therefore
$$
\boxed{E(x,y,t)=E_0\exp[i(kn_g\sin\theta\,x-\omega t)]\exp[-k\sqrt{n_g^2\sin^2\theta-n_w^2}\,y].}
$$
It propagates in phase parallel to the interface but decays normally. Here $E_0$ is the transmitted field amplitude at the interface; relating it to the incident amplitude requires the appropriate polarization-dependent <Fresnel equations>. If the symbol $k$ instead denotes the incident glass <wavenumber>, replace every vacuum $k$ in the formula by $k/n_g$; equivalently $k_x=k\sin\theta$ and $\kappa=k\sqrt{\sin^2\theta-(n_w/n_g)^2}$. This states the wavenumber convention left implicit in the source.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The excitation rate in the weak, unsaturated <fluorescence> regime is proportional to the time-averaged squared <electric field> amplitude. Thus the illumination profile relevant to excitation is
$$
\boxed{I(y)=I(0)e^{-2\kappa y}=I(0)e^{-y/d_I},\qquad d_I=\frac1{2\kappa}=\frac{\lambda_0}{4\pi\sqrt{n_g^2\sin^2\theta-n_w^2}}.}
$$
The amplitude decay length $1/\kappa$ is twice the intensity decay length. This profile is often called the evanescent excitation intensity; it does not imply a propagating normal energy flux through an ideal lossless totally reflecting interface.

<Total internal reflection fluorescence microscopy> restricts excitation to a shallow layer adjacent to the glass, typically on the scale of hundreds of nanometres for visible wavelengths and suitable angles. Fluorophores near the basal <cell membrane> are excited while much of the cytoplasm and bulk solution remain dark. The resulting reduction in out-of-focus fluorescence and autofluorescence improves the signal-to-background ratio for individual molecules, allows camera-based parallel observation, and reduces unnecessary excitation of deeper regions. Its limitation is the same selectivity: it does not image arbitrary molecules deep inside a cell. This near-interface selectivity was directly demonstrated in cell–substrate contact imaging.<Cell-substrate TIRF demonstration (1981)>[evanescent excitation and reduction of fluorescence background at cell–substrate contacts]{ref}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Under the simplest interpretation, each spot contains two equally bright, independently bleaching <green fluorescent protein> emitters. Losing one halves the fluorescence and losing the other removes it. A stable <protein dimer> carrying one active label per subunit is consistent with this result. The conclusion is two active emitters in the observed spot; it does not by itself rule out unresolved coincident proteins, incomplete fluorescent maturation or blinking. Permanent membrane binding makes loss by dissociation less plausible, but lateral motion out of the observation region would still need experimental control.<Membrane-protein photobleaching counting study (2007)>[stepwise photobleaching as a subunit-counting measurement and its labeling assumptions]{ref}

Let their independent <photobleaching> times be $T_A,T_B\sim\operatorname{Exp}(k_b)$. Then $t_1=\min(T_A,T_B)$ has rate $2k_b$, while the memoryless remaining lifetime $t_2-t_1$ has rate $k_b$ and mean $1/k_b$, matching the stated single-emitter calibration. The <two-emitter photobleaching completion time> is the maximum, not one single exponential and not a sum of two equal-rate exponential variables. Its <cumulative distribution function> is
$$
P(t_2\leq t)=P(T_A\leq t,T_B\leq t)=(1-e^{-k_bt})^2\qquad(t\geq0).
$$
Differentiating gives its <probability density function>:
$$
\boxed{f_{t_2}(t)=2k_be^{-k_bt}(1-e^{-k_bt})=2k_b(e^{-k_bt}-e^{-2k_bt}),\quad t\geq0,}
$$
with zero density for $t<0$. It integrates to one, and its mean is $1/(2k_b)+1/k_b=3/(2k_b)$. This uses two active fluorophores at the start of observation, constant illumination and independent irreversible bleaching with the calibrated rate.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Cells interconvert several forms of <free energy>. Chemical concentration differences and the chemical potentials of metabolites can drive reactions, including <ATP hydrolysis>. The usable free-energy difference is that between reactants and products under the actual cellular conditions; breaking a chemical bond alone does not release energy without the rest of the reaction. <Oxidation-reduction reactions> transfer electrons down an electrochemical potential difference and can drive ion pumping in an <electron transport chain>. Light absorption can create excited states and charge separation in <photosynthesis>, eventually supporting chemical synthesis.

An <electrochemical gradient> stores free energy in both an ion concentration difference and a <membrane potential>. <Chemiosmosis> couples downhill ion flow to <ATP synthesis by chemiosmosis>; ATP-powered pumps can run the conversion the other way. <Molecular motors> and other mechanochemical enzymes convert chemical free energy into mechanical work or elastic strain, while ion gradients can also power transport and rotary motion. These couplings conserve total energy but dissipate some free energy as heat. Equilibrium thermal fluctuations alone cannot sustain directional work without a maintained nonequilibrium resource.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Use number concentrations and write the <electrochemical potential> of a sodium ion as $\widetilde\mu=\mu^\circ+k_{\rm B}T\ln c+e\psi$. Define the inward <sodium-motive force> as available work per positive charge for transport from outside to inside. With $V_m=\psi_i-\psi_o$,
$$
\boxed{\mathcal E_{\rm Na}=\frac{\widetilde\mu_o-\widetilde\mu_i}{e}=\frac{k_{\rm B}T}{e}\ln\frac{c_o}{c_i}-V_m.}
$$
Its negative is the signed inward free-energy cost per charge; stating this direction removes a common sign ambiguity.

Long-open sodium channels establish electrochemical equilibrium, $k_{\rm B}T\ln(c_{i0}/c_o)+eV_{m0}=0$. This does not generally require equal concentrations: impermeant counterions can support a nonzero equilibrium <membrane potential>. Let $n_0=c_{i0}V$. For a large fixed-concentration external reservoir, after pumping $n$ ions out, $c_i(n)=(n_0-n)/V$ and $V_m(n)=V_{m0}-ne/C$. The <sodium export cost from a capacitive vesicle> for exporting another ion is
$$
\Delta G_{\rm Na}(n)=k_{\rm B}T\ln\frac{c_oV}{n_0-n}-eV_{m0}+\frac{ne^2}{C}=\boxed{\frac{ne^2}{C}+k_{\rm B}T\ln\frac{n_0}{n_0-n}}.
$$
The initial-equilibrium relation was used in the last step. The first term is the incremental electrical cost and the second is the change in chemical cost from their initial balanced values.

If the intended initial reference is uncharged with $V_{m0}=0$ and $c_{i0}=c_o$, then $n_0=c_oV$ and the requested form in the listed variables is
$$
\boxed{\Delta G_{\rm Na}(n)=\frac{ne^2}{C}+k_{\rm B}T\ln\frac{c_o}{c_o-n/V},\qquad 0<n<c_oV.}
$$
That equal-concentration reference is an additional assumption. For example, $c_{i0}=2c_o$ and $V_{m0}=-(k_{\rm B}T/e)\ln2$ also give zero initial sodium flux, but a different ion inventory. Thus the open-channel condition alone cannot determine $n_0$ from $c_oV$. If concentrations are molar rather than number densities, use the corresponding <Avogadro constant> conversion and $RT$ for molar free energies. Treating the capacitor charge discretely gives a charging-energy increment $(n+1/2)e^2/C$ rather than the differential $ne^2/C$; the distinction is negligible for the thousands of ions used next.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the initial equal-concentration, zero-potential reference, or equivalently quote component changes relative to the initial electrochemical equilibrium. At $n=n_0/2=5000$, with $C=10^{-16}\,\mathrm F$,
$$
\boxed{\Delta G_{\rm elec}=\frac{5000e^2}{C}=1.28\times10^{-18}\,\mathrm J=8.01\,\mathrm{eV}.}
$$
At room temperature $T=300\,\mathrm K$, the chemical component is
$$
\boxed{\Delta G_{\rm chem}=k_{\rm B}T\ln2=2.87\times10^{-21}\,\mathrm J=0.0179\,\mathrm{eV}.}
$$
The electrical cost dominates by about $447$. The associated change in <membrane potential> is $-ne/C=-8.01\,\mathrm V$. Such a large voltage is a consequence of the ideal fixed-capacitance, perfectly impermeable model; an actual lipid membrane would not generally remain intact and nonconducting over this entire extrapolation.

The electrical component is a straight line through zero, $ne^2/C$. The chemical component, $k_{\rm B}T\ln[n_0/(n_0-n)]$, starts at zero, increases convexly, and diverges as $n\to n_0^-$ because the remaining internal concentration vanishes. Their sum lies above both: nearly electrical over most of the stated range, but eventually diverging with the chemical term. For a nonzero initial equilibrium potential, the separate *absolute* electrical and chemical components have opposite constant offsets; the total curve and the quoted changes remain the same. The final few ions require a discrete-particle treatment rather than a literal continuum divergence.

\Image[2015-s12-sodium-pumping-free-energy.png]
{title=Electrical, chemical and total sodium export costs in the ideal vesicle model}

== S16
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume ideal, nondegenerate particles in local thermodynamic equilibrium, ground-state atomic hydrogen, no molecules, and a common temperature. Let $\lambda_j=h/\sqrt{2\pi m_jk_BT}$ be the thermal <de Broglie wavelength>. Choose zero energy for a separated stationary proton and electron, so a neutral atom has ground-state energy $-R$. The classical <chemical potentials> are
$$
\mu_e=k_BT\ln\frac{n_e\lambda_e^3}{g_e},\quad \mu_p=k_BT\ln\frac{n_p\lambda_p^3}{g_p},\quad \mu_H=-R+k_BT\ln\frac{n_H\lambda_H^3}{g_H}.
$$
Chemical equilibrium for $H\rightleftharpoons p+e$ requires $\mu_H=\mu_p+\mu_e$. Rearranging gives
$$
\frac{n_en_p}{n_H}=\frac{g_eg_p}{g_H}\frac{\lambda_H^3}{\lambda_e^3\lambda_p^3}e^{-R/(k_BT)}.
$$
Including electron and proton spin degeneracy, $g_e=2$, $g_p=2$ and $g_H=4$ when hyperfine splittings are neglected. Hence the statistical factor is one. Also $m_H\simeq m_p$, so $\lambda_H^3/\lambda_p^3\simeq1$. <Charge neutrality> gives $n_p=n_e$, and therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{n_e^2}{n_H}=\frac{(2\pi m_ek_BT)^{3/2}}{h^3}\exp\left(-\frac{R}{k_BT}\right).}
$$
This is the specified ground-state <Saha equation>. Keeping excited atomic partition functions or a different spin-counting convention can change a prefactor, so its convention must be stated rather than silently adding a factor of two. The left side has dimensions of a number density, not a dimensionless electron-to-neutral ratio.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $N=n_H+n_p$ be the conserved number density of hydrogen nuclei, whether ionized or bound, and $x=n_p/N=n_e/N$ the <ionization fraction>. The ideal <gas pressure> counts neutral atoms, free protons and free electrons:
$$
P=Nk_BT(1+x),\qquad \frac{x^2}{1-x}=\frac{S(T)}N,\quad S(T)=\frac{(2\pi m_ek_BT)^{3/2}}{h^3}e^{-R/(k_BT)}.
$$
At low temperature $x\simeq0$ and $P\simeq Nk_BT$. Heating through the ionization region increases the particle count as well as their thermal energy, giving a steeper rise. At high temperature $x\simeq1$, so $P\simeq2Nk_BT$ and the asymptotic slope is twice the low-temperature slope. There is a smooth ionization crossover, not a pressure discontinuity.

The held graph is legible. Representative readings are $P(5000\,\mathrm K)\simeq6.8\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and $P(15000\,\mathrm K)\simeq41.4\,\mathrm{Pa}$, with readout uncertainties of roughly $0.3$–$0.5\,\mathrm{Pa}$ and about $100\,\mathrm K$. They give $N\simeq P/(k_BT)$ on the neutral branch and $N\simeq P/(2k_BT)$ on the ionized branch, respectively. Both are consistent with
$$
\boxed{N\simeq(1.0\pm0.05)\times10^{20}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}.}
$$
The error is a conservative graph-reading estimate, not a fitted experimental confidence interval.

For that density, the <Saha equation> gives $x\simeq0.50$ at $9000\,\mathrm K$, $x\simeq0.81$ at $10000\,\mathrm K$, and $x\simeq0.9992$ at $15000\,\mathrm K$. Differentiating the ionization relation gives
$$
x'=\frac{x(1-x)}{2-x}\left(\frac{3}{2T}+\frac{R}{k_BT^2}\right),\qquad P'=Nk_B(1+x+Tx').
$$
The calculated maximum slope occurs near $9.25\times10^3\,\mathrm K$, consistent with the source's visibly steepest region at roughly $(9.3\pm0.4)\times10^3\,\mathrm K$. This checks the actual slope rather than identifying it without justification with exactly half ionization.

Taking the neutral ground-state gas as the zero of internal binding energy, the matter <energy density> is translational kinetic energy plus ionization energy:
$$
u=\frac32Nk_BT(1+x)+NxR.
$$
At $15000\,\mathrm K$, with $R=13.6\,\mathrm{eV}$, these terms are about $62$ and $218\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$, giving
$$
\boxed{u\simeq2.8\times10^2\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}.}
$$
Rest energies are excluded. A separate equilibrium radiation field, if the gas were in a blackbody cavity, would add its own radiation energy density; it is not part of this gas-only estimate.

\Image[2015-s16-saha-pressure.png]
{title=Calculated hydrogen pressure and ionization crossover at two conserved nucleus densities}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Increasing the conserved nucleus density to $10N$ multiplies the low-temperature neutral-gas slope and high-temperature ionized-gas slope by ten. However, at a fixed intermediate temperature, $x^2/(1-x)=S(T)/(10N)$ is smaller, so a smaller fraction is ionized. The curved transition therefore moves to higher temperature; the new graph is not simply ten times the old graph throughout the crossover.

At a fixed chosen <ionization fraction>, $S(T)$ must increase tenfold. Locally this requires
$$
\Delta T\simeq\frac{\ln10}{3/(2T)+R/(k_BT^2)},
$$
which is of order $10^3\,\mathrm K$ in this region. Directly evaluating the same <Saha equation> at $10^{21}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ moves the maximum pressure slope from about $9250$ to $10560\,\mathrm K$. The two asymptotic slopes still have ratio two, because each hydrogen nucleus still contributes one particle when neutral and two when fully ionized.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The electron <Debye length> is the scale over which a weak electrostatic disturbance is screened. Take the ions as an immobile neutralizing background and the electrons as an isothermal <Boltzmann distribution>. For a small potential $\phi$,
$$
n_e(\phi)=n_0e^{e\phi/(k_BT)}\simeq n_0\left(1+\frac{e\phi}{k_BT}\right),\qquad \rho\simeq-\frac{n_0e^2}{k_BT}\phi.
$$
<Poisson's equation> then becomes $\nabla^2\phi=\phi/\lambda_D^2$ away from the perturbing source, with
$$
\boxed{\lambda_D=\sqrt{\frac{\epsilon_0k_BT}{n_ee^2}}.}
$$
A test charge has a screened potential proportional to $e^{-r/\lambda_D}/r$. For fully static screening with mobile ions, inverse squared Debye lengths from all species add; the displayed expression is the electron scale used below.

The electron <plasma frequency> is the natural collective oscillation frequency when electrons are displaced from a nearly fixed ion background. Displace a uniform electron fluid by $\xi$ along a slab normal. The charge separation produces the restoring electric force $-n_ee^2\xi/\epsilon_0$ on an electron. Hence $m_e\ddot\xi=-n_ee^2\xi/\epsilon_0$ and
$$
\boxed{\omega_p=\sqrt{\frac{n_ee^2}{\epsilon_0m_e}}.}
$$
It sets a response time $1/\omega_p$ and the cold unmagnetized electromagnetic cutoff frequency. These collective effects arise from long-range electric fields, even when individual binary collisions are infrequent.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Define the <plasma parameter> here as the number of electrons in an electron Debye sphere,
$$
N_D=\frac{4\pi}{3}n_e\lambda_D^3.
$$
Some texts instead use $n_e\lambda_D^3$, or its inverse as a coupling parameter. A weakly coupled collective plasma has $N_D\gg1$; fluctuations from individual nearby particles are small compared with the collective description.

A Coulomb collision means the cumulative deflection or relaxation produced by many small-angle encounters, not necessarily a hard contact between two particles. The <Coulomb logarithm> $\ln\Lambda_C\simeq\ln(b_{\max}/b_{\min})$ summarizes the impact-parameter range, with $b_{\max}$ of order $\lambda_D$. A process is collisionless when its timescale is much shorter than the appropriate collisional relaxation time, or its length scale is much smaller than a corresponding mean free path. It still contains collective interactions.

For fully ionized ions of charge $Ze$, a thermal electron–ion momentum-relaxation rate has the scaling
$$
\nu_{ei}\sim\frac{Zn_ee^4\ln\Lambda_C}{\epsilon_0^2m_e^{1/2}(k_BT)^{3/2}},\qquad \boxed{t_{\rm col}\sim\frac{N_D}{Z\ln\Lambda_C\,\omega_p}},
$$
up to numerical factors depending on the Debye-sphere and collision-time conventions. More explicitly, the Maxwellian rate used below gives $\nu_{ei}/\omega_p\simeq0.089\,Z\ln\Lambda_C/N_D$, or $t_{\rm col}\omega_p\simeq11.3N_D/(Z\ln\Lambda_C)$. Specifying this factor prevents mixing a small coupling parameter with a large Debye-sphere population.<NRL Plasma Formulary (2019)>[Fundamental Plasma Parameters and Coulomb Collisions: electron thermal and collision-rate conventions]{ref}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Use collisional <inverse bremsstrahlung> in a weakly coupled, unmagnetized plasma. The specified density is $n_e=10^{26}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, and $k_BT=1000\,\mathrm{eV}$. The electron <Debye length> is $2.35\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm m$, the <plasma frequency> is $5.64\times10^{14}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and the laser angular frequency is $\omega=2\pi c/\lambda=1.79\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Thus $X=\omega_p^2/\omega^2\simeq0.099<1$: the plasma is underdense and a propagating wave exists.

Estimate $b_{\min}$ as the larger of the classical Coulomb-deflection length $Ze^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0k_BT)$ and the electron quantum length $\hbar/\sqrt{m_ek_BT}$. Both are about $9\times10^{-12}\,\mathrm m$ here, giving $\ln\Lambda_C\simeq8$. With the usual thermal collision convention,
$$
\nu_{ei}\simeq2.91\times10^{-6}\frac{n_e[\mathrm{cm^{-3}}]Z\ln\Lambda_C}{T_e[\mathrm{eV}]^{3/2}}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\simeq4.4\times10^{11}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.
$$
The $Z$ factor follows from $n_iZ^2=n_eZ$.<NRL Plasma Formulary (2019)>[Coulomb collision-rate normalization and thermal parameters; densities in cubic centimetres and temperatures in eV]{ref}

The weakly collisional <Drude model> gives $\epsilon_r=1-\omega_p^2/[\omega(\omega+i\nu_{ei})]$ for fields proportional to $e^{-i\omega t}$. Expanding at $\nu_{ei}\ll\omega$ yields a real refractive index $n_r=\sqrt{1-X}\simeq0.949$ and intensity attenuation coefficient
$$
\alpha_I=2\operatorname{Im}k\simeq\frac{\nu_{ei}}{c}\frac{X}{\sqrt{1-X}}.
$$
Consequently the intensity e-folding absorption length is
$$
\boxed{L_I=\frac{c\sqrt{1-X}}{\nu_{ei}X}\simeq6.6\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm m,}
$$
i.e. of order a centimetre. Taking a Coulomb logarithm between about 5 and 10 gives roughly $5$–$10\,\mathrm{mm}$. The electric-field amplitude decay length is twice this intensity length. This estimate assumes a uniform slab thick enough to define an absorption length and neglects gradients, nonlinear quiver heating and other absorption mechanisms.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

For a sinusoidal laser field of peak amplitude $E_0$, an electron's peak nonrelativistic quiver speed is $v_q=eE_0/(m_e\omega)$. Choose the one-component electron thermal speed $v_T=\sqrt{k_BT/m_e}=1.33\times10^7\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. The vacuum-equivalent laser intensity is $I=\epsilon_0cE_0^2/2$, so the onset $v_q\sim v_T$ occurs at
$$
I_{\rm th}\simeq\frac{\epsilon_0c}{2}\left(\frac{m_e\omega v_T}{e}\right)^2,\qquad \boxed{I_{\rm th}\simeq2.4\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{W\,cm^{-2}}.}
$$
Using $\sqrt{2k_BT/m_e}$ as the thermal-speed convention doubles this estimate; its order of magnitude is the physical content. Once the quiver is comparable to the thermal speed, a collision rate computed for an undriven thermal distribution is no longer adequate.

Relativistic motion begins when the normalized field $a_0=eE_0/(m_ec\omega)$ is of order one. Thus
$$
\boxed{I_{\rm rel}\simeq\frac{\epsilon_0c}{2}\left(\frac{m_ec\omega}{e}\right)^2\simeq1.2\times10^{18}\,\mathrm{W\,cm^{-2}}.}
$$
The ratio $I_{\rm rel}/I_{\rm th}=m_ec^2/(k_BT)\simeq511$ checks the separation of the two regimes. These estimates use peak field and cycle-averaged vacuum intensity. The small refractive-index and interface-transmission corrections in this underdense plasma change only the few-percent prefactor, below the accuracy intended here.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Take $\mathbf B=B\hat{\mathbf z}$. The magnetic <Lorentz force> gives
$$
\dot v_x=\Omega v_y,\qquad \dot v_y=-\Omega v_x,\qquad \dot v_z=0,\qquad \Omega=\frac{qB}{m}.
$$
Thus $v_x+iv_y=(v_x(0)+iv_y(0))e^{-i\Omega t}$, while $v_z$ is constant. Integrating the transverse velocity gives a circle of radius $r_L=v_\perp/|\Omega|$ about a fixed guiding centre; adding $z(t)=z_0+v_\parallel t$ gives a <helical motion in a uniform magnetic field>. The <magnetic force> does no work, so the speed and kinetic energy remain constant. The handedness reverses with the sign of $q$. If $v_\parallel=0$ the helix degenerates to a circle; if $v_\perp=0$ it is a straight line parallel to the field.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Seek a constant transverse <guiding-centre drift> $\mathbf v_d$ whose magnetic <Lorentz force> balances the applied force. Define
$$
\mathbf v_d=\frac{\mathbf F\times\mathbf B}{qB^2}.
$$
Since $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf B=0$, the vector triple-product identity gives $q\mathbf v_d\times\mathbf B=-\mathbf F$. Writing $\mathbf v=\mathbf v_d+\mathbf u$ therefore reduces the full equation to $m\dot{\mathbf u}=q\mathbf u\times\mathbf B$, the ordinary gyromotion equation. Its transverse orbit is circular in the drifting frame, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v_{\rm gc}=\mathbf v_d=\frac1q\frac{\mathbf F\times\mathbf B}{B^2}.}
$$
Averaging over a gyroperiod removes the transverse oscillatory velocity and leaves this drift, together with any constant parallel velocity. For an electric force $\mathbf F=q\mathbf E$, this becomes the charge-independent $\mathbf E\times\mathbf B/B^2$ drift; a charge-independent force instead gives opposite drift directions for opposite charge signs.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

A purely toroidal <magnetic field> is stronger on the inner side of the torus and its field lines are curved. The resulting <magnetic gradient drift> and <curvature drift> move ions and electrons vertically in opposite directions. Charge separation can then produce an electric field whose <E cross B drift> moves the plasma across the nominal toroidal confinement region. Closing field lines into circles therefore does not by itself guarantee confinement.

A <tokamak> combines a strong externally produced toroidal field with a poloidal field supplied largely by toroidal plasma current. Field lines wind helically around nested <magnetic flux surfaces>. A particle following a field line samples different vertical and radial parts of the torus, so the systematic displacements associated with the toroidal geometry can compensate over its orbit rather than accumulating as in the pure-toroidal case. The poloidal field also provides the magnetic structure needed for force balance. This improves confinement but does not eliminate all orbit losses or transport: trapped particles, collisions, turbulence and magnetohydrodynamic instabilities remain important. Maintaining the plasma current and suitable field-line winding is part of the confinement problem.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The supplied confinement criterion alone fixes a product, not a unique current or machine size. Choose a representative ion density $n_i=10^{20}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. Then
$$
\boxed{\tau\gtrsim\frac{10^{20}}{n_i}\,\mathrm s\simeq1\,\mathrm s.}
$$
For $n_i=10^{19}$ to $10^{21}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, the same criterion gives $\tau\gtrsim10$ to $0.1\,\mathrm s$. These are illustrative density choices, not additional source data.

For a current estimate, assume a large-aspect-ratio circular tokamak with major radius $R_0=3\,\mathrm m$, minor radius $a=1\,\mathrm m$, toroidal field $B_\phi=5\,\mathrm T$ and edge <tokamak safety factor> $q_s\simeq3$. The geometrical winding relation and <Ampère's law> give
$$
q_s\simeq\frac{aB_\phi}{R_0B_\theta},\qquad B_\theta(a)\simeq\frac{\mu_0I_p}{2\pi a},\qquad I_p\simeq\frac{2\pi a^2B_\phi}{\mu_0R_0q_s}.
$$
For the stated assumptions,
$$
\boxed{I_p\simeq2.8\times10^6\,\mathrm A,}
$$
so toroidal plasma currents of order megaamperes are a natural scale. As a consistency check at $T\sim10^8\,\mathrm K$, quasineutral singly charged heavy hydrogen has total thermal pressure $p\simeq2n_ik_BT\simeq2.8\times10^5\,\mathrm{Pa}$. With $5\,\mathrm T$, $2\mu_0p/B^2\simeq0.028$, so the assumed magnetic pressure exceeds the thermal pressure. The particular current depends on geometry and field-line pitch; it cannot be inferred from the confinement product alone.

== S18
{c}
{scope}
{title2=2S98}
{title2=2S99}

Worked notes are available in <Advanced Quantum Mechanics: S18 Exam 2015 Solutions>. The requested Q1 amplitude sketch is supplemented below; the analytic worked parts remain available in those notes.

Alternate S18 question extract: https://www-thphys.physics.ox.ac.uk/people/AndreiStarinets/short_options_exam_2015.pdf. It carries the printed identifiers 2S98/2S99 and matches this option's three question cores, with wording and footer variations.

=== 1
{scope}

==== 6
{scope}

===== Solution

For real $x=ka$, taking the <modulus> squared of the amplitude gives the <half-line delta-well amplitude enhancement>
$$
\boxed{|A(x)|^2=\frac{\lambda^2x^2}{(1-\cos2x)^2+(\lambda x-\sin2x)^2}.}
$$
The supplied <resonance poles> have real parts $x_n\simeq n\pi(1+\lambda/2+\lambda^2/4)$ and negative imaginary parts $-\gamma_n\simeq-(\lambda n\pi/2)^2$ for fixed low $n$ at $\lambda\ll1$.<Advanced Quantum Mechanics: S18 Exam 2015 Solutions>[Problem 1, PDF pp.5–6: pole expansion]{ref} Linearizing the denominator at a simple pole gives
$$
|A(x)|^2\simeq\frac{\lambda^2x_n^2}{|D'(p_n)|^2[(x-x_n)^2+\gamma_n^2]},\qquad D(x)=1-e^{2ix}+i\lambda x.
$$
Thus the sketch has narrow, tall peaks with a locally <Lorentzian line shape>, just above positive integer multiples of $\pi$. Their half-widths are $\gamma_n$, and the leading peak heights are $4/(\lambda^2n^2\pi^2)$. In this fixed-low-mode approximation, increasing $n$ broadens and lowers the peaks. Exactly at $x=n\pi\ne0$, the value is one. At zero it has the finite limit $\lambda^2/(2-\lambda)^2$ for $\lambda\ne2$.

The peaks represent long-lived <standing waves> between the wall and the attractive <delta potential>. Despite the source's transmission-coefficient terminology, $|A|^2$ is an internal amplitude factor and can exceed one. The interior <probability current> is zero, and the reflected <probability current> equals the incident magnitude; the hard wall passes no transmitted flux.

\Image[2015-s18-delta-well-amplitude-sketch.png]
{title=Internal standing-wave resonances and the Lorentzian line shape of the first peak}

== S19
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== 1
{scope}

====== Solution

Use transverse coordinates $(x,x')$ and define a positive signed focal length as focusing in that plane. A thin <quadrupole magnet> of length $\ell$ and signed gradient $G$ has integrated strength $1/f=|e|G\ell/p_0$; the electron charge and chosen field orientation are absorbed into the sign of $G$. Its <beam transfer matrix> is
$$
\boxed{Q(f)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1/f&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad f=\frac{p_0}{|e|G\ell}.}
$$
For total electron <energy> $E_0$, $p_0=\sqrt{E_0^2-m_e^2c^4}/c$, so $f\simeq E_0/(|e|cG\ell)$ in the ultrarelativistic limit. The other transverse plane has the opposite signed focal length. A <quadrupole magnet> therefore focuses one plane while defocusing the other; the longitudinal <magnetic field> component is negligible in this paraxial idealization.

===== 2
{scope}

====== Solution

At fixed gradient and magnet length, $f\propto p$. If $E$ is total <energy>,
$$
\frac{d\ln p}{d\ln E}=\frac{E^2}{E^2-m_e^2c^4}=\frac1{\beta_0^2},\qquad
\boxed{f(E)=f_0\left(1+\frac\delta{\beta_0^2}\right)+O(\delta^2).}
$$
Here $\beta_0=v_0/c$, not one of the <Courant-Snyder parameters>. For ultrarelativistic electrons this becomes $f\simeq f_0(1+\delta)$, or $1/f\simeq(1-\delta)/f_0$: higher-energy particles are bent and focused less strongly. If the symbol $E$ instead denotes <kinetic energy> $K$, the coefficient is $(K+mc^2)/(K+2mc^2)$, approaching $1/2$ in the nonrelativistic limit. Stating the energy convention avoids applying the ultrarelativistic result to a slow beam.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $D(d)=\begin{pmatrix}1&d\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$ be the drift <beam transfer matrix>. Start immediately before the first lens and end immediately before its counterpart in the next <FODO cell>. Matrices act on column vectors from right to left. With $A=1/f_1$ and $B=1/f_2$, where both focal lengths are signed in the chosen plane,
$$
\boxed{M=DQ(f_2)DQ(f_1)=
\begin{pmatrix}
1-2dA-dB+d^2AB&2d-d^2B\\
-A-B+dAB&1-dB
\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The two drifts account for both gaps. Direct multiplication gives $\det M=1$ and
$$
\frac12\operatorname{tr}M=1-d(A+B)+\frac{d^2AB}{2}.
$$
For the equal-strength focusing-defocusing case, $f_1=f>0$ and $f_2=-f$:
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}1-d/f-d^2/f^2&2d+d^2/f\\-d/f^2&1+d/f\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \operatorname{tr}M=2-\frac{d^2}{f^2}.
$$
Choosing a different start within the cell gives a conjugate matrix, with the same trace and eigenvalues. If $f_2$ is specified as a positive magnitude of a defocusing lens, its signed value in these formulas is $-f_2$.

==== c
{scope}

===== 1
{scope}

====== Solution

For a real uncoupled, lossless transverse map, $\det M=1$. Bounded nondegenerate oscillatory motion requires complex-conjugate <eigenvalues> $e^{\pm i\mu}$, equivalently
$$
\boxed{|\operatorname{tr}M|<2.}
$$
Its <Courant-Snyder parameters> give
$$
M=\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\mu+\alpha_T\sin\mu&\beta_T\sin\mu\\
-\gamma_T\sin\mu&\cos\mu-\alpha_T\sin\mu
\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\beta_T>0,\quad\beta_T\gamma_T-\alpha_T^2=1.
$$
The positive quadratic form $\gamma_Tx^2+2\alpha_Txx'+\beta_Tx'^2$ is invariant, so repeating the cell cannot produce secular growth. At trace $\pm2$ a nontrivial Jordan block instead produces linear growth; only the exceptional maps $M=\pm I$ remain bounded at those exact boundaries. The criterion must hold in both transverse planes. For the symmetric <FODO cell>, both traces are $2-d^2/f^2$, giving
$$
\boxed{0<d/|f|<2,\qquad \sin(\mu/2)=d/(2|f|).}
$$

===== 2
{scope}

====== Solution

For ultrarelativistic particles with fixed magnet settings, $A(\delta)=A_0(1-\delta)$ and $B(\delta)=B_0(1-\delta)$. Hence the half-trace is
$$
\boxed{C(\delta)=C_0+\delta\left[d(A_0+B_0)-d^2A_0B_0\right]+O(\delta^2),\quad
C_0=1-d(A_0+B_0)+\tfrac12d^2A_0B_0.}
$$
Off-energy motion is stable when $|C(\delta)|<1$, separately in each transverse plane. Where $\sin\mu_0\ne0$, its first-order phase shift is
$$
\Delta\mu=-\frac{d(A_0+B_0)-d^2A_0B_0}{\sin\mu_0}\,\delta.
$$
For equal opposite strengths,
$$
\boxed{\mu(\delta)=\mu_0-2\tan(\mu_0/2)\delta+O(\delta^2).}
$$
Thus positive momentum error reduces the focusing and phase advance; negative error moves toward the overfocused unstable boundary. The unexpanded symmetric stability condition is $|f_0|(1+\delta)>d/2$, with $1+\delta>0$, within the ultrarelativistic energy scaling. Its natural <chromaticity> per cell is $d(\mu/2\pi)/d\delta=-\tan(\mu_0/2)/\pi$. Away from the ultrarelativistic limit replace the displayed first-order energy error by $\delta/\beta_0^2$. Linearizing at a stability boundary is singular and needs separate treatment.

=== 2
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

For an ultrarelativistic electron of <Lorentz factor> $\gamma=E/(m_ec^2)=3000/0.511=5870.84$, the <synchrotron critical frequency> gives $\varepsilon_c=\hbar\omega_c=3\hbar c\gamma^3/(2\rho)$. Thus, using the supplied constants,
$$
\boxed{\rho=\frac{3\hbar c\gamma^3}{2\varepsilon_c}=7.47\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The angle-integrated spectral power per unit photon energy is proportional to $x\int_x^\infty K_{5/3}(u)du$, with $x=\varepsilon/\varepsilon_c$. It peaks at $x\simeq0.286$, so
$$
\boxed{\varepsilon_{\rm peak}\simeq2.29\,\mathrm{keV}.}
$$
This is a power spectrum per linear energy interval; power per logarithmic interval and photon-count spectra have different peaks. The <magnetic rigidity> condition is $p=|e|B\rho$, with $pc\simeq E$, giving
$$
\boxed{B\simeq\frac{E}{|e|c\rho}=1.34\,\mathrm T.}
$$
The critical energy sets a broad spectral scale, not a maximum allowed photon energy.<Synchrotron Radiation in the Geant4 Physics Reference Manual by Geant4 Collaboration>[critical energy and angle-integrated synchrotron spectrum]{ref}

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

For a ring whose total bending angle $2\pi$ is supplied by dipoles with the computed radius, the single-electron energy loss per turn is
$$
U_0=\frac{4\pi}{3}\frac{r_em_ec^2\gamma^4}{\rho}
=9.57\times10^5\,\mathrm{eV}.
$$
A stored current $I$ corresponds to $I/|e|$ electron turns per second: the number of stored electrons times the revolution rate. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{I}{|e|}U_0=(0.100\,\mathrm A)(9.57\times10^5\,\mathrm V)
\simeq9.57\times10^4\,\mathrm W.}
$$
This is the whole beam's radiated power, not the power accepted by one beamline. If the specified radius describes only one dipole and the other bends are different, the radiation integral is needed instead:
$$
U_0=\frac23r_em_ec^2\gamma^4\oint\frac{ds}{\rho(s)^2}.
$$
The numerical result uses the standard equal-radius-bend assumption; the ring circumference, including straight sections, is not required. The RF system must replenish this loss to maintain stored beam energy.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

Near the critical photon energy, the characteristic vertical divergence of <synchrotron radiation> is $\sigma_\theta\simeq1/\gamma=1.70\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{rad}$ in the approximation requested. Ignoring electron source size and divergence, the rms vertical width $10\,\mathrm m$ downstream is
$$
\sigma_y\simeq L\sigma_\theta=1.70\,\mathrm{mm}.
$$
The full aperture width is $10\,\mathrm{mm}$, so its half-width is $5\,\mathrm{mm}=2.94\sigma_y$. It therefore passes almost all the central $8\,\mathrm{keV}$ photon beam. A Gaussian angular approximation would give
$$
\boxed{\mathcal T\simeq\operatorname{erf}\left(\frac{5\,\mathrm{mm}}{\sqrt2\,\sigma_y}\right)\simeq0.9967.}
$$
The exact synchrotron angular distribution is not Gaussian, so this number is an illustrative containment estimate, not an exact throughput calculation. A finite aperture cannot transmit every photon in a distribution with tails. The assumed infinite horizontal aperture removes horizontal fan clipping.

==== d
{scope}

===== Solution

Below the critical photon energy, radiation is emitted over a wider angular cone. The usual low-frequency characteristic-angle estimate is
$$
\sigma_\theta(\varepsilon)\sim\frac1\gamma
\left(\frac{\varepsilon_c}{\varepsilon}\right)^{1/3},
$$
up to an order-one angular-width convention. At $\varepsilon=0.012\,\mathrm{eV}$ this gives
$$
\boxed{\sigma_\theta\sim1.49\times10^{-2}\,\mathrm{rad},\qquad
\sigma_y\sim0.149\,\mathrm m.}
$$
The aperture should therefore be enlarged **vertically**. A full width of order $0.30\,\mathrm m$ covers $\pm1$ characteristic width; approximately $0.90\,\mathrm m$ covers $\pm3$ and gives containment comparable with the preceding X-ray estimate in the same Gaussian model. The original $10\,\mathrm{mm}$ width would pass only roughly $\operatorname{erf}[0.005/(\sqrt2\,0.149)]\simeq2.7\%$ in that model. Equivalent optics could instead reduce the spot at a relocated aperture, at the expense of angular acceptance. These estimates concern geometric acceptance; THz coherence and bunch structure require further beam data.

=== 3
{scope}

==== a
{scope}

===== Solution

An RF cavity stores an oscillating <electromagnetic field>. Its longitudinal <electric field> does <work> on a correctly phased bunch, with energy gain $\Delta E=qV_{\rm RF}\mathcal T\sin\phi_s$ for a chosen phase convention and transit-time factor $\mathcal T$. The bunch arrival time is synchronized so successive gaps accelerate rather than decelerate it. The <magnetic force> guides and focuses the beam but does no work.

A <linear accelerator> uses new accelerating structures along its path; cell lengths and timing accommodate the particle speed, and each bunch normally traverses a given section once. A circular <synchrotron> reuses the same RF cavities each revolution. Its synchronous phase supplies both acceleration and radiation replacement; RF frequency or harmonic synchronization and bending-field strength follow the required orbit and particle speed. Longitudinal phase stability captures nearby particles into RF buckets.

For very high-energy **electron and positron** colliders, a linear geometry avoids repeated synchrotron losses: at fixed radius, loss per turn scales as $E^4/m^4$. This is not a statement that all high-energy colliders are preferably linear. The LHC was already a circular proton collider in the 2015 context; the proton's much larger mass greatly suppresses radiation at the same energy. Circular electron machines can also be competitive at lower energies. Magnet limits, luminosity, cost and beamstrahlung enter any real comparison.

==== b
{scope}

===== Solution

Conventional RF cavities accelerate beams with controlled phase, energy spread and transverse <beam emittance>. Their material surfaces limit sustainable gradients to order $10$–$100\,\mathrm{MV\,m^{-1}}$, depending on technology and operating conditions. Mature RF systems already reached multi-GeV and much higher beam energies in 2015. The 2013 ILC baseline proposed $250\,\mathrm{GeV}$ electrons colliding with $250\,\mathrm{GeV}$ positrons, approximately $31.5\,\mathrm{MV\,m^{-1}}$ superconducting cavities and a roughly $31\,\mathrm{km}$ facility. These were design parameters, not an operating collider.https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.6327[2013 ILC Technical Design Report, Volume 1: baseline 500 GeV centre-of-mass design]{ref}

In <plasma wakefield acceleration>, an intense laser displaces plasma electrons and excites a longitudinal charge-density wave. A suitably injected electron bunch gains energy from that wave, with fields of order tens to hundreds of $\mathrm{GV\,m^{-1}}$. A concrete result available before this examination was $4.2\,\mathrm{GeV}$ from a $9\,\mathrm{cm}$ capillary, with $6\%$ rms energy spread, $6\,\mathrm{pC}$ bunch charge and $0.3\,\mathrm{mrad}$ rms divergence. These values characterize that experiment, not every laser-plasma beam; divergence alone is not an emittance measurement.https://physics.aps.org/featured-article-pdf/10.1103/PhysRevLett.113.245002[2014 experiment, abstract and first page: measured energy, spread, charge, divergence and capillary length]{ref}

A collider concept stages many separately driven plasma modules, with fresh laser pulses, interstage capture and refocusing, controlled injection, and a final collision focus. Dephasing and pump depletion limit a single stage. Although the accelerating sections could be much shorter than ILC linacs, a useful collider also requires high repetition rate and wall-plug efficiency, reproducible low energy spread, preserved normalized emittance, stable timing, high bunch charge and acceptable beam-beam effects. Positron acceleration is an additional challenge because an electron-optimized plasma wake need not focus positrons. Large gradient alone does not demonstrate the luminosity or beam quality of an RF collider.

==== c
{scope}

===== Solution

In the cold unmagnetized linear plasma model, the refractive-index relation is $n^2=1-\omega_p^2/\omega_L^2$, with
$$
\omega_p^2=\frac{n_ee^2}{\epsilon_0m_e}.
$$
The critical density occurs at $\omega_p=\omega_L=2\pi c/\lambda_L$. Thus
$$
\boxed{n_{\rm crit}=\frac{\epsilon_0m_e}{e^2}\left(\frac{2\pi c}{\lambda_L}\right)^2
\simeq1.12\times10^{27}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}=1.12\times10^{21}\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}},}
$$
for $\lambda_L=1\,\mu\mathrm m$. The corresponding frequency is
$$
\boxed{\omega_p\simeq1.88\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}},\qquad
f_p=\omega_p/(2\pi)\simeq3.0\times10^{14}\,\mathrm{Hz}.}
$$
A propagating conventional laser wake generally uses an underdense plasma, rather than this cutoff density. Strong relativistic laser motion, collisions or a magnetized plasma modify this simple critical-density condition.

==== d
{scope}

===== Solution

A suitable accelerator-science example is <strong focusing>. The apparent technical contradiction is that increasing a <quadrupole magnet>'s focusing strength improves confinement in one transverse plane while worsening it in the other. Abandoning that magnet entirely loses its strong focusing action.

An alternating-gradient lattice separates the two actions in space: a focusing quadrupole in one plane is followed, after a drift, by one with the opposite sign, and the pattern repeats. In the other plane their roles reverse. The symmetric thin-lens <FODO cell> has the same trace $2-d^2/f^2$ in both planes, so both are stable for $0<d/|f|<2$, despite each individual quadrupole defocusing one plane. The noncommuting sequence of drift and lens maps is what makes net confinement possible.

In a TRIZ interpretation, this resolves the contradiction by **spatial separation and periodic alternation** of opposing functions, rather than demanding that one static element satisfy both. The useful result is smaller beam envelopes and magnet apertures for a suitable lattice. This is a retrospective design analysis; it does not claim that the historical inventors actually followed TRIZ. The matrix calculation supplies a test of the proposed resolution rather than just attaching a slogan to an invention.

== S25
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The <tropopause> is the transition between the lower, often convecting <troposphere> and the more stably stratified atmosphere above, where the decrease of temperature with altitude weakens or reverses. Its definition concerns the thermal profile, not a fixed universal altitude. An <optical depth> is a dimensionless path-integrated extinction, $\tau_\nu=\int\kappa_\nu\rho\,ds$ for mass extinction coefficient $\kappa_\nu$; without emission into the ray its direct intensity is attenuated by $e^{-\tau_\nu}$.

In <hydrostatic equilibrium>, the upward pressure force balances weight:
$$
\boxed{\frac{dp}{dz}=-\rho g=-\frac{gp}{R_aT(z)}.}
$$
The second equality uses the <ideal gas law> with specific gas constant $R_a$. For constant $T$ and $g$,
$$
p(z)=p(z_0)e^{-(z-z_0)/H},\qquad \boxed{H=R_aT/g.}
$$
For variable temperature the corresponding result is $p(z)=p(z_0)\exp[-\int_{z_0}^z g/(R_aT(z'))dz']$. The fixed-temperature derivative does not require the entire column to be isothermal.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume a well-mixed ideal gas with constant $g,c_p$, no latent heat, and sufficiently strong surface heating to maintain dry <convection> near the surface. An adiabatically displaced unit mass has specific volume $V=1/\rho$, so the stated relation and <hydrostatic equilibrium> give
$$
c_p\,dT=V\,dp=-g\,dz,
\qquad \boxed{\Gamma_d=-\frac{dT}{dz}=\frac g{c_p}\simeq9.8\,\mathrm{K\,km^{-1}}.}
$$
This is the dry neutral lapse rate; infrared absorption alone does not guarantee that a radiative profile is steep enough to trigger convection. Combining the same equations with the <ideal gas law> yields $T\propto p^{R_a/c_p}$ along that adiabat.

High above the main infrared-emitting region, optical depth to space becomes small. A transparent-to-sunlight atmosphere has no substantial direct solar heating there; a thin grey layer absorbs the upwelling planetary infrared flux $Q=(1-\alpha)S_0/4$ and emits from both faces. In the optically thin radiative-equilibrium limit,
$$
\sigma T_{\rm skin}^4\simeq Q/2,
\qquad \boxed{T_{\rm skin}\simeq\left[\frac{(1-\alpha)S_0}{8\sigma}\right]^{1/4}.}
$$
The factor four in $Q$ is projected-area versus whole-sphere averaging; the additional factor two counts emission upward and downward by the tenuous layer. For Earth-like $S_0\simeq1360\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$ and $\alpha\simeq0.30$, this is about $214\,\mathrm K$. Thus temperature approaches a height-independent skin value as $\tau\to0$, rather than continuing the dry adiabat to zero. This is an asymptotic grey-atmosphere approximation, not a proof that an entire finite stratosphere is exactly isothermal.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $q$ be the mass mixing ratio of the uniformly mixed infrared absorbers, and let $k$ be their constant mass absorption coefficient. Hydrostatic integration gives downward <optical depth> $\tau=kqp/g$. The useful fixed-tropopause-optical-depth approximation requires a sufficiently optically thick lower column, unchanged absorbed sunlight and upper radiative profile in optical-depth coordinates, and the same convective lapse law after the perturbation. Then the matching to the radiative region occurs at the same $\tau_t$ and temperature $T_t$. Doubling all those absorbers gives
$$
\boxed{p_t'\simeq p_t/2.}
$$
Assume also that the surface pressure is unchanged. A dry adiabat satisfies $T_t/T_s=(p_t/p_s)^\kappa$ with $\kappa=R_a/c_p=0.287$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{T_s'}{T_s}=2^{0.287}=1.2201,\qquad
\Delta T_s\simeq0.2201T_s\simeq63\,\mathrm K}
$$
for an initial Earth-like surface temperature $288\,\mathrm K$. Its first-order logarithmic estimate, $\Delta T_s\simeq\kappa T_s\ln2$, is about $57\,\mathrm K$; the finite-doubling formula is preferable because the perturbation is large. This estimate is conditional on fixed $\tau_t,T_t$ and dry convective adjustment, not on carbon dioxide's measured spectral behavior.

There is also a consistency limitation to the most literal opacity model. A self-contained plane-parallel grey atmosphere with constant mass opacity has $T^4\propto\tau+2/3$ and $\tau\propto p$. Its radiative logarithmic gradient is
$$
\nabla_{\rm rad}=\frac{d\ln T}{d\ln p}=\frac{\tau}{4(\tau+2/3)}<\frac14<\kappa.
$$
It is therefore subadiabatic for these dry heat capacities, so it does not by itself establish the assumed deep dry convective column. The preceding warming is the intended fixed-matching-level, convectively prescribed estimate. A fully self-consistent model needs opacity varying with pressure, other heating structure or another closure to supply that convection; the two statements should not be silently identified.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

The actual Earth response to doubling <carbon dioxide> is expected to be **much smaller** than this roughly $60\,\mathrm K$ all-grey-absorbers estimate. Carbon dioxide absorbs in selected spectral bands rather than doubling opacity across the entire thermal spectrum, and doubling it does not double the other infrared absorbers. Saturated band centres and overlap with water absorption further distinguish it from uniform opacity multiplication. Changes in water vapour, clouds and the lapse rate introduce important <climate feedbacks>, but do not justify treating the real atmosphere as the stipulated dry, wavelength-independent absorber. The idealized number is therefore not an estimate of real-world <equilibrium climate sensitivity>.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Use a positive stabilizing feedback coefficient: the net flux is $N=F_{\rm ext}-\lambda\Delta T$. The <Planck feedback> increases outgoing thermal radiation as temperature rises and therefore contributes positively to $\lambda$. <Water-vapor feedback> raises infrared opacity as a warmer atmosphere holds more water, tending to reduce $\lambda$; <ice-albedo feedback> also tends to reduce it as bright ice is lost. These signs depend on the explicitly stated convention.

For doubled carbon dioxide forcing $F_{2\times}>0$, equilibrium gives
$$
\boxed{\Delta T_{2\times}=F_{2\times}/\lambda.}
$$
A symmetric positive interval $[\lambda_0-\sigma_\lambda,\lambda_0+\sigma_\lambda]$ maps to
$$
\boxed{\frac{F_{2\times}}{\lambda_0+\sigma_\lambda}\leq\Delta T_{2\times}\leq
\frac{F_{2\times}}{\lambda_0-\sigma_\lambda}.}
$$
The upward excursion is larger than the downward one because inversion is nonlinear. If the interval reaches zero, it supplies no finite upper bound on equilibrium warming within this linear stable model.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

Take the logarithmic forcing law with its normalization fixed by the given doubling value. Let $x$ be the positive magnitude of cooling and $f$ the positive magnitude of the earlier negative forcing:
$$
f=\frac{3.75}{\ln2}\ln\frac{280}{C},\qquad x=1.0\,\mathrm K.
$$
At $C=212\,\mathrm{ppm}$, $f_0=1.5051\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. Equilibrium then yields
$$
\boxed{\lambda_0=f_0/x_0=1.505\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-1}},\qquad
\Delta T_{2\times,0}=3.75\frac{x_0}{f_0}=2.492\,\mathrm K.}
$$
The concentration endpoints $185$ and $244\,\mathrm{ppm}$ imply forcing magnitudes $2.242$ and $0.7445\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$, approximately $f_0\pm0.75$. Define normalized variables $u=f/f_0$ and $v=x/x_0$. Independent approximately Gaussian uncertainties give the one-standard-deviation covariance ellipse
$$
\boxed{\frac{(u-1)^2}{0.50^2}+\frac{(v-1)^2}{0.50^2}\simeq1.}
$$
Its centre is $(1,1)$ and its principal directions are the two coordinate axes. Warming is $2.492(v/u)\,\mathrm K$, so rays from the origin have constant inferred warming. The bounding rays tangent to this ellipse have slopes $m$ satisfying $(1-m)^2=\tfrac14(1+m^2)$, or
$$
m_-\simeq0.4514,\qquad m_+\simeq2.2153.
$$
This gives the useful asymmetric one-parameter profile estimate
$$
\boxed{\Delta T_{2\times}\simeq2.5\,\mathrm K,\qquad
\text{approximate }1\sigma\text{ interval }1.1\text{–}5.5\,\mathrm K.}
$$
The concentration data supply only marginal intervals, not a full likelihood. This result explicitly approximates their logarithmic forcing uncertainty as Gaussian. Linear error propagation would instead give fractional uncertainty $\sqrt{0.5^2+0.5^2}=0.707$, or roughly $2.49\pm1.76\,\mathrm K$, and misses the large reciprocal asymmetry. A unit-radius two-dimensional covariance ellipse itself contains about $39\%$ of a bivariate Gaussian, not $68\%$; the quoted interval uses the one-parameter $\Delta\chi^2=1$ profile/ratio construction, rather than claiming that ellipse is a $68\%$ joint region.

\Image[2015-s25-forcing-temperature-and-feedback.png]
{title=Climate sensitivity from uncertain forcing and a quadratic feedback fold}
{description=Original model plots: the left panel shows the normalized independent-uncertainty ellipse and tangent constant-sensitivity rays. The right panel plots the assumed quadratic restoring flux, its stable and unstable branches, and the finite forcing at which those equilibria merge. The fold is a property of this toy model, not an observed carbon-dioxide threshold.}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

The quadratic coefficient must have units $\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-2}}$; the printed $\mathrm{K^{-1}}$ makes its term dimensionally incompatible with a radiative flux. Interpret its numerical value as $\lambda_2=0.05\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-2}}$, with $\lambda_1=1.5\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}\,K^{-1}}$. The total imbalance is
$$
N=F_{\rm ext}-\lambda_1\Delta T+\lambda_2\Delta T^2.
$$
A quadrupling gives $F_{\rm ext}=2(3.75)=7.5\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}}$. The two equilibrium roots are
$$
\Delta T_\pm=\frac{\lambda_1\pm\sqrt{\lambda_1^2-4\lambda_2F_{\rm ext}}}{2\lambda_2},
\qquad \boxed{\Delta T_-=6.34\,\mathrm K,\quad\Delta T_+=23.66\,\mathrm K.}
$$
For positive heat capacity, stability requires $\partial N/\partial T=-\lambda_1+2\lambda_2\Delta T<0$. Thus only the lower root is stable. A <saddle-node bifurcation> occurs at
$$
\boxed{F_{\rm crit}=\frac{\lambda_1^2}{4\lambda_2}=11.25\,\mathrm{W\,m^{-2}},\quad
\Delta T_{\rm crit}=\frac{\lambda_1}{2\lambda_2}=15\,\mathrm K.}
$$
The logarithmic forcing relation gives $C_{\rm crit}=280\,2^{11.25/3.75}=2240\,\mathrm{ppm}$. Strict linear stability holds below this concentration on the lower branch; the equality is marginal and is the supremum, not another strictly stable equilibrium. Sustained forcing above it causes warming without a balancing equilibrium in this quadratic model. At such large changes its local feedback expansion can fail, and other physics can intervene; it does not prove unlimited real-planet warming or locate a measured Earth runaway threshold.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Put $\vartheta=T-T_0$ and assume $C>0$, $\lambda>0$. The <linear differential equation> for a constant forcing step is
$$
\dot\vartheta+\frac\lambda C\vartheta=\frac{F_0}{C},\qquad\vartheta(0)=0.
$$
Multiplying by the integrating factor $e^{\lambda t/C}$ and integrating from zero to $t$ gives
$$
\boxed{T(t)=T_0+\frac{F_0}{\lambda}(1-e^{-k_Tt}),\qquad k_T=\frac\lambda C.}
$$
The response initially grows at rate $F_0/C$ and tends to the equilibrium warming $F_0/\lambda$. Heat capacity per unit area and radiative-feedback units make $k_T$ an inverse time.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

For logarithmic forcing $F=a\ln(C_a/C_{a0})$, where $C_a$ denotes atmospheric carbon-dioxide concentration rather than heat capacity, $\dot F=(a/C_a)\dot C_a$. Approximately constant $\alpha$ in $\dot F=\alpha E$ therefore requires a roughly constant conversion from emissions to persistent atmospheric burden and a concentration range small enough to hold $a/C_a$ fixed. Carbon removal must be negligible on the modeled horizon, or be replaced by a fixed retained fraction; a realistic multiscale carbon-cycle response generally violates the permanent-step approximation.

A one-tonne pulse at time $t_e$ is $E(t)=\delta(t-t_e)$ in tonne-normalized units. It gives a persistent forcing step $F=\alpha\Theta(t-t_e)$. Convolving that step with the thermal response yields
$$
\boxed{T(t)-T_0=\frac\alpha\lambda\left[1-e^{-k_T(t-t_e)}\right]\Theta(t-t_e).}
$$
There is no instantaneous temperature jump for finite $C$, although forcing jumps. Both persistence and constant coefficients are deliberate simplifications, rather than an assertion that all emitted carbon dioxide remains airborne forever.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Insert the pulse response into $D=\beta(T-T_0)$ and write $u=t'-t_e$. For a positive discount rate $k_D$,
$$
\mathrm{PV}=\frac{\beta\alpha}{\lambda}\int_0^\infty(1-e^{-k_Tu})e^{-k_Du}\,du
=\frac{\beta\alpha}{\lambda}\left(\frac1{k_D}-\frac1{k_D+k_T}\right).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{PV}=\frac{A}{k_D(k_D+k_T)},\qquad
A=\frac{\alpha\beta k_T}{\lambda}=\frac{\alpha\beta}{C}.}
$$
With $k_D=0.03\,\mathrm{year^{-1}}$, $k_T=0.05\,\mathrm{year^{-1}}$ and the supplied one-tonne normalization of $A$,
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{PV}=\frac{0.4}{0.03(0.03+0.05)}\simeq167\,\mathrm{USD\,tonne^{-1}}.}
$$
This is valued at the time of emission because the discount factor is relative to that date. Valuing an emission at a future date from an earlier reference date would require an additional discount factor. The infinite-horizon integral converges for $k_D>0$ under the assumed persistent forcing.

==== 4
{scope}

===== Solution

Let $Q$ be annual emissions abatement in tonnes per year. The given marginal abatement cost is
$$
\mathrm{MAC}(Q)=\frac{200}{10^{10}}Q\quad\mathrm{USD\,tonne^{-1}}.
$$
Under the stipulated global, stationary, unconstrained cost-benefit model, the optimal interior abatement equates that marginal cost to the avoided marginal damage of approximately $166.7\,\mathrm{USD}$ per tonne. Thus
$$
\boxed{Q_*\simeq8.33\times10^9\,\mathrm{tonnes\,year^{-1}}.}
$$
Total mitigation expenditure is the area under the marginal-cost curve, not its endpoint value times all abated tonnes:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal C(Q_*)=\int_0^{Q_*}\mathrm{MAC}(Q)\,dQ
=\tfrac12(166.7)Q_*\simeq6.94\times10^{11}\,\mathrm{USD\,year^{-1}}.}
$$
The result also presumes baseline emissions permit this much reduction. Rational policy need not reproduce it: different discounting, damage uncertainty and risk aversion, income weights, omitted ecosystem impacts, adaptation and local air-quality benefits alter valued benefits; technology learning and adjustment constraints alter costs. Separate rational governments may abate less because some benefits accrue abroad, while cooperative policy can internalize those benefits. Future climate or technology changes also undermine a fixed present marginal-damage curve. These are changes in the optimization problem, not evidence of irrational decision-makers.

== S27
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

<Newton's first law> says that a body retains uniform rectilinear motion, including rest as the zero-velocity case, unless impressed <forces> change that motion. Its physical content includes the existence of a common class of <inertial frames>: free bodies do not each require an unrelated frame chosen just to straighten their own trajectory. Uniformity refers to the inertial time parameter, and straightness to the spatial structure of those frames. Everyday slowing under friction is compatible with this claim because it involves an interaction, not an exception to inertia.

<Newton's second law> relates the impressed resultant <force> to change of <momentum>. In modern continuous-force notation,
$$
\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}=\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}},\qquad \mathbf p=m\mathbf v,
$$
so for constant <mass> it becomes $m\mathbf a=\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}$. An impulsive version is $\Delta\mathbf p=\int\mathbf F\,dt$. The change is directed along the impressed resultant, not necessarily along the body's current <velocity>. The original wording concerns change of quantity of motion; the differential equation is its modern continuous-time formulation.<Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica by Isaac Newton>[1687, Definition II and Axioms, or Laws of Motion, Laws I–II; distinguish momentum from velocity]{ref}

These are substantive physical claims only when <mass>, interactions and the inertial structure are constrained independently. If “force” merely abbreviates whatever equals $m\mathbf a$ in any arbitrarily chosen coordinates, the second law by itself predicts nothing. Actual applications combine the law with independently testable interaction laws, vector addition of forces, initial conditions and a specification of the inertial frames.

==== i
{scope}

===== Solution

Showing that <acceleration> is unchanged by a uniform boost is necessary, but it is not enough to establish the <Galilean covariance of Newton's second law>. One must specify how time, positions, masses, forces and any background physical systems transform, and show that the actual force laws retain their form.

For a constant spatial rotation $R$, boost velocity $\mathbf U$ and translations, write
$$
t'=t+b,\qquad \mathbf x'=R\mathbf x-\mathbf U t+\mathbf a.
$$
Differentiation gives $\mathbf v'=R\mathbf v-\mathbf U$ and $\mathbf a'=R\mathbf a$. Assuming invariant inertial <mass>,
$$
\frac{d\mathbf p'}{dt'}=R\frac{d\mathbf p}{dt}.
$$
Consequently the equation has the same form if the resultant physical <force> satisfies $\mathbf F'=R\mathbf F$. For a pure boost, this reduces to $\mathbf F'=\mathbf F$. More explicitly, if a force is represented by a function of positions, velocities and other physical data, those arguments and data must transform so that its primed value is $R$ times the old value. It is this condition on the complete dynamical model, not the typography of $\mathbf F=m\mathbf a$, that establishes covariance.

An instantaneous inverse-square interaction depending on the separation of two particles passes this test: their common boost cancels from the separation, and a rotation rotates the force vector. A resistance law $\mathbf F=-\zeta(\mathbf v-\mathbf u)$ also passes when the medium's <velocity> $\mathbf u$ transforms with the body. In contrast, keeping $\mathbf F=-\zeta\mathbf v$ in every boosted frame while silently resetting the same medium to rest changes the physical problem. That model has not been shown covariant by noting $\mathbf a'=\mathbf a$.

Likewise, the full <Maxwell equations> are not made Galilean covariant merely because some equation of particle motion is written as force equals mass times acceleration. The covariance of a framework has to be checked together with its interaction laws. Newton's own Corollary V expresses the corresponding equivalence of uniformly translating, nonrotating systems for the mechanics under consideration.<Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica by Isaac Newton>[1687, Corollary V following the Laws of Motion; the modern boost calculation above makes its dynamical conditions explicit]{ref}

==== ii
{scope}

===== Solution

Within an already specified <inertial frame>, with constant <mass>, substituting zero resultant <force> into <Newton's second law> does give zero <acceleration>. In that limited mathematical sense, force-free uniform motion is a special case. The reason for retaining <Newton's first law> is that its physical role is broader than this substitution.

The second law does not, just by its algebra, identify the coordinate systems in which acceleration measures the response to physical interactions. A free body described in coordinates $x'=x-\tfrac12At^2$ has $\ddot x'=-A$, despite experiencing no impressed interaction. If the second law is restricted to inertial frames, the first law supplies a criterion for that restriction and asserts that a common inertial structure exists. If instead one introduces a fictitious force $-mA$ to preserve the equation in the accelerated coordinates, that coordinate-dependent bookkeeping should not be confused with a newly discovered physical interaction.

Nor can the first law simply be used as a circular definition in which “free” means “unaccelerated” and “inertial” means “whatever makes the chosen body free”. The framework requires empirical identification of interactions and a mutually consistent class of free-body trajectories. Removing an influence or improving isolation should make the same inertial description work for many bodies, not merely one fitted trajectory.

Thus the two laws have distinguishable jobs: the first articulates the force-free inertial background; the second quantifies departures caused by impressed forces. A modern axiomatization may build that background into its spacetime structure and then omit a separately numbered first law. That is a legitimate reorganization, but it incorporates the first law's content rather than obtaining all of it from the equation $\mathbf F=m\mathbf a$. Newton states the laws separately in the opening axioms.<Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica by Isaac Newton>[1687, Laws I–II and the preceding Scholium on time, space and motion]{ref}

=== 2
{scope}

==== Solution

Einstein's distinctive achievement in 1905 was a reorganization of the physical interpretation and foundations of the theory, not the invention of every transformation formula or every previously known optical effect. He took the equivalence of inertial descriptions and a light-propagation postulate as principles from which to construct a general kinematics of rods, clocks and electromagnetic processes, without assigning explanatory work to an observable state of rest of the ether.<On the Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies by Albert Einstein>[1905, introduction and sections 1–3: magnet–conductor asymmetry, clock synchronization, the two postulates and the coordinate transformation]{ref}

The nineteenth-century comparison matters. <Hendrik Antoon Lorentz>'s 1895 theory explicitly describes matter moving through a stationary ether and introduces transformed quantities to relate moving and stationary descriptions. Such work supplied genuine physical insight and important mathematical antecedents; it should not be dismissed as an absence of relativity reasoning.<Attempt of a Theory of Electrical and Optical Phenomena in Moving Bodies by Hendrik Antoon Lorentz>[1895, introduction and sections II and IV: stationary ether, moving matter and transformed electromagnetic descriptions]{ref} His 1904 paper, a useful immediate pre-Einstein comparison rather than a nineteenth-century publication, begins from electron theory and adds assumptions about moving material systems. It already seeks a systematic explanation of the failure to detect motion, rather than an unrelated hypothesis for every experiment.<Electromagnetic phenomena in a system moving with any velocity smaller than that of light by Hendrik Antoon Lorentz>[1904, sections 1–3 and 10–11: programme of the theory and assumptions about the deformation of moving matter]{ref}

The conceptual change can be seen by asking what a moving clock's “local time” represents. In an ether-based explanation there may remain a privileged time relative to which the moving apparatus is dynamically distorted. In Einstein's formulation the times assigned by identically constructed, appropriately synchronized clocks in each inertial system enter the physical description on the same footing. One should not infer a physically privileged inertial system simply from calling one set of coordinates stationary.

This also changes the scope of the explanation. A special electron model might account for the behaviour of one class of instruments. A relativity principle constrains any acceptable dynamics of measuring devices and matter: if one kind of clock revealed absolute uniform motion, the principle would fail. The result is a framework within which more detailed constructive theories can be developed, rather than a substitute for all such theories.

A careful assessment therefore credits both continuity and innovation. Transformation mathematics and contraction hypotheses had antecedents; the operational treatment of distant time, the equal standing of inertial descriptions, and the use of general principles to unify kinematics and electrodynamics form the characteristic 1905 synthesis. It does not follow that an empirically indistinguishable preferred-frame ontology is logically impossible. What is removed is its necessity in this physical formulation. Nor should the separate 1905 mass–energy paper be silently treated as a section of the June relativity paper.

=== 3
{scope}

==== Solution

Yes. Differential ageing between twins who separate and reunite can be calculated entirely within <special relativity>, provided gravity is negligible and ideal clock behaviour is specified. The theory is not confined to particles that remain inertial throughout their histories: accelerated timelike trajectories can be described in a single inertial coordinate system in flat <Minkowski spacetime>.

The invariant quantity recorded by an ideal clock is its <proper time>,
$$
\tau=\int\sqrt{1-\frac{v(t)^2}{c^2}}\,dt.
$$
Take one twin at rest in an inertial laboratory. Let the other travel out at speed $v$ for laboratory time $T/2$ and return at the same speed for $T/2$, idealizing the turnaround as very short. On reunion,
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{home}}=T,\qquad \tau_{\mathrm{traveller}}=T\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2},
\qquad \Delta\tau=T\left(1-\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}\right)>0.
$$
Both times are compared at the same reunion event, so this is not a disagreement about observing a distant clock or choosing a convention for distant synchronization. Einstein's 1905 discussion already considers clock transport along a closed path; the proper-time integral supplies the general modern calculation.<On the Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies by Albert Einstein>[1905, section 4, transported-clock discussion; the displayed integral and two-leg example are the modern formulation]{ref}

The apparent paradox applies reciprocal inertial time dilation as if both twins used one inertial frame from departure to reunion. They do not. The traveller changes inertial frames at the turn, and the associated assignment of simultaneity to distant events changes with it. Reciprocal time dilation for each separate inertial leg is compatible with unequal total proper times along the two worldlines.

Acceleration marks the asymmetry of this itinerary, but is not a universal extra term that locally slows a clock by an amount proportional to its acceleration. Smooth the turnaround and integrate along the resulting worldline: the answer depends on the full path. In the short-turnaround limit the extra proper time during the turn becomes small while the differential ageing of the long legs remains finite. One can even compare successive inertial travellers who relay clocks or records, avoiding the mistaken inference that general relativity is needed to describe the inertial legs.

The <clock hypothesis> adds the physical idealization that a suitable clock records metric proper time without additional acceleration-dependent damage or internal disturbance. A real biological twin or laboratory clock can of course suffer such disturbances; those are questions about the device's dynamics, not a logical contradiction in the relativistic prediction. General relativity is needed if significant spacetime curvature or gravity is part of the experiment, not merely because a worldline bends in a flat spacetime diagram.

=== 4
{scope}

==== Solution

Bell's warning concerns the order of explanation. A formal change of coordinates can be mistaken for a physical mechanism, or for a proof that ordinary questions about material rods and clocks have become meaningless. In the cited essay he recommends first following a concrete model's equations of motion, so that changes in a moving system's shape and period are understood before a general correspondence between frames is used. The phrase quoted in the question occurs in note 10, following the discussion of a classical charged-particle model.<How to teach special relativity by John Stewart Bell>[1976; collected version, chapter 9, pages 67–77 and especially note 10 on pages 79–80; the warning is attached to the passage from a concrete dynamical example to a general change-of-variables argument]{ref}

A useful independent diagnostic is to ask whether a proposed account predicts an actual stress. Consider two ships with identical acceleration schedules in one inertial laboratory and a fragile thread between them. Their laboratory separation remains fixed. Once both coast at speed $v$, the separation of the attachment points measured in their final common rest frame is $\gamma L$, where $L$ was the fixed laboratory separation. A thread whose unstressed rest length is $L$ must stretch to span those points. For a sufficiently fragile thread it breaks. Saying merely that all contraction is a matter of description misses a mechanical consequence of the specified history. Conversely, length contraction is not an additional universal force pulling every object shorter regardless of its constraints: the stress response depends on its material dynamics and acceleration programme.

The same methodological point applies to clocks. Their ability to realize the relativistic time standard requires compatible physical laws. Calling a variable a new time coordinate does not by itself explain why an atomic transition, an oscillator and a material measuring instrument behave consistently with that variable. Lorentz-covariant dynamics can connect these descriptions without introducing a second set of observable clock readings.

Bell's lesson need not be inflated into a rejection of Einstein's principles or of Minkowski geometry. His comparison expressly recognizes the power of the principle approach and does not require adopting an empirically preferred ether frame. The defensible caution is that covariance, observable physical behaviour and an ontology of spacetime are different claims. Geometrical explanations are useful when their connection to matter and measurement is understood; premature philosophising begins when a coordinate transformation is made to settle those further issues without argument.

=== 5
{scope}

==== Solution

There is an important structural difference, but the issue needs two distinctions. The freedom to change distant clock labels is not the same as the absence of a physically preferred simultaneity relation; and an inability to measure a one-way signal speed without synchronization is not by itself a proof that all proposed temporal structures are mere conventions.

In a standard <Einstein synchronisation> procedure, a clock at A sends a light signal at $t_1$, receives its return at $t_3$, and assigns the reflection event at B the time $(t_1+t_3)/2$. More generally one could assign
$$
t_B=t_1+\epsilon(t_3-t_1),\qquad 0<\epsilon<1.
$$
For a fixed spatial distance $d$ with round-trip duration $2d/c$, the two assigned one-way speeds become $c/(2\epsilon)$ and $c/[2(1-\epsilon)]$. Their reciprocal travel times still add to $2d/c$. Thus the two-way measurement alone does not establish the midpoint rule. Einstein presents synchronization as a definition supported by consistency conditions, rather than as a direct comparison of already synchronized distant clocks.<On the Electrodynamics of Moving Bodies by Albert Einstein>[1905, section 1, outgoing and returning light signals and the definition of synchronous stationary clocks]{ref}

Both <Newtonian mechanics> and <special relativity> allow coordinated relabellings such as $t_\kappa=t+\kappa x/c$. They can be used as nonstandard coordinates if the equations and interpretations of the transformed quantities are changed consistently. A mere relabelling cannot change a reunion of clocks or another local coincidence. Conversely, insisting that the familiar equations must retain their usual simple component form while changing only clock labels is not a neutral test of conventionality.

The difference is in the invariant structures of the standard theories. Newtonian spacetime includes an absolute time, whose equal-time slices are preserved by <Galilean transformations>. It therefore contains one simultaneity structure shared by its inertial observers. Its instantaneous interaction laws, where adopted, are formulated relative to that time. Ordinary relativistic spacetime contains no analogous observer-independent foliation. <Einstein synchronisation> defines simultaneity relative to an inertial observer; different inertial observers generally assign different distant events to the same time. This difference survives the freedom to rewrite either theory in awkward coordinates.

It does not follow that any arbitrary synchronization is equally natural relative to the full structure of special relativity. An inertial observer together with the Minkowski metric picks out the usual orthogonal simultaneity hyperplanes, and ideal slow clock transport supplies a physical comparison under assumptions about clock behaviour. A conventionalist must say which such structures and operational assumptions are being held fixed. Likewise, a Newtonian theorist must not confuse positing absolute time with having a theory-independent operational measurement of it.

The best answer is therefore qualified: both theories admit synchronization conventions at the level of coordinate assignment, but their standard physical accounts privilege simultaneity differently. Newtonian absolute simultaneity and the relativistic absence of a shared inertial foliation are genuine theoretical differences. Whether an alternative clock assignment is merely a convention or a rival physical claim depends on whether it only relabels the same structure or adds, removes or changes physical structure.

== S28
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== Solution

In <Bohmian mechanics>, the corpuscles supply an actual configuration of matter, not a further branch of the <wave function>. Their positions and the guiding wave together specify the physical state. In a common nonrelativistic spinless formulation, the wave obeys the <Schrödinger equation> while the actual configuration $Q=(Q_1,\ldots,Q_N)$ obeys
$$
\dot Q_k=\frac{\hbar}{m_k}\operatorname{Im}\frac{\nabla_k\Psi}{\Psi}(Q,t),
$$
where the expression is applied away from nodes of $\Psi$. These are coupled in the sense that the wave guides the configuration; in the standard theory the wave's Schrödinger evolution does not receive a reciprocal source term from the actual particle positions. This is not a model of classical little balls carrying every ordinary observable as a pre-existing intrinsic attribute.

The point of adding a configuration is especially clear in a measurement. A wave may evolve into several macroscopically separated pointer packets. The apparatus's actual particle configuration lies in one packet, so there is a definite pointer record even though the full wave has not undergone fundamental collapse. The corpuscles thereby connect the mathematical description with the location of actual apparatus, records and observers. <David Bohm>'s original papers provide a theory of individual processes and analyse measurement in this spirit.<A Suggested Interpretation of the Quantum Theory in Terms of Hidden Variables I by David Bohm>[1952, Physical Review 85, pages 166–179: additional variables and continuous individual dynamics]{ref}<A Suggested Interpretation of the Quantum Theory in Terms of Hidden Variables II by David Bohm>[1952, Physical Review 85, pages 180–193: measurement analysis and correlations between separated systems]{ref}

For a subsystem, inserting the actual environment configuration into the universal wave gives a <conditional wave function>, schematically $\psi(x,t)\propto\Psi(x,Y(t),t)$. If the branches are suitably disjoint in the environment's configuration coordinates and remain dynamically isolated, the occupied branch supplies an <effective collapse in Bohmian mechanics>. The unoccupied branches are not simply erased by definition: before permanent separation, wave packets can recombine and affect interference. The conditional and effective wave-function constructions make this distinction precise.<Quantum Equilibrium and the Role of Operators as Observables in Quantum Theory by Detlef Dürr and others>[sections 2.1–2.3, especially Conditional and Effective Wave Functions; conditional wave functions need not always obey an autonomous subsystem Schrödinger equation]{ref}

The <Born rule> also needs an account. The guidance current is $j_k=(\hbar/m_k)\operatorname{Im}(\Psi^*\nabla_k\Psi)$, and both $|\Psi|^2$ and an ensemble of guided configurations satisfy the associated <continuity equation>. Hence an initial <quantum equilibrium> distribution $\rho=|\Psi|^2$ is preserved. Equivariance supports the ordinary statistical predictions, but the guidance law alone does not prove that an arbitrarily prepared ensemble has that distribution.

The corpuscles are therefore neither detectors that a conscious observer adds to the theory nor dispensable labels attached after the statistics have been calculated. They constitute a <primitive ontology> that provides definite configurations, trajectories and records. Whether this is preferable to a wave-only ontology is a further explanatory judgement; its empirical equivalence in quantum equilibrium does not make the two accounts identical in their claims about what exists.

=== 2
{scope}

==== Solution

An apparent collapse is the transition from predicting several possible recorded outcomes to describing a particular recorded result. Whether that transition is a physical discontinuity, a conditional state update or an effective description depends on what the <wave function> is taken to represent. The practical success of collapse-based calculation does not by itself settle that interpretive question.

The difficulty is exposed by <unitary time evolution>. An ideal apparatus that reliably measures a basis has
$$
|0\rangle|A_{\mathrm{ready}}\rangle\longmapsto|0\rangle|A_0\rangle,\qquad
|1\rangle|A_{\mathrm{ready}}\rangle\longmapsto|1\rangle|A_1\rangle.
$$
Linearity then makes a superposed input evolve as
$$
(a|0\rangle+b|1\rangle)|A_{\mathrm{ready}}\rangle
\longmapsto a|0\rangle|A_0\rangle+b|1\rangle|A_1\rangle.
$$
This is an entangled state, not automatically one pointer packet or the other. A separate projection rule would instead select one recorded outcome, with <Born rule> weights, and assign its corresponding conditional state.

<Quantum decoherence> explains much of the practical appearance of a classical record. Interaction with an environment correlates the packets with nearly orthogonal environment states. The subsystem's <reduced density matrix> then has very small interference terms between the pointer alternatives. Local experiments on that subsystem approximately agree with a diagonal mixture. But this is an <improper mixture>: the total state can remain a pure superposition. Taking a <partial trace> has not selected one term of the total state. Suppression of observable interference and the occurrence of a unique absolute outcome are distinct claims.

There is a <measurement problem> if one simultaneously insists that the wave function is the complete physical description, that its evolution is universally unitary, and that a measurement has exactly one absolute outcome of the ordinary recorded kind. The ideal model shows why simply applying the same unitary law to a larger apparatus does not make that combination work. Nor does calling collapse a change in knowledge solve the problem for someone who has already taken the wave function to be a complete ontic state.

Different interpretations revise different parts of this package. <Bohmian mechanics> adds an actual configuration and obtains effective subsystem collapse while the total wave evolves unitarily. The <many-worlds interpretation> retains the total superposition and treats each record as definite relative to its decohering branch. A stochastic collapse theory instead modifies the physical dynamics. Everett's original proposal explicitly separates his relative-state account from an additional collapse postulate.<Relative State Formulation of Quantum Mechanics by Hugh Everett III>[1957, introduction and measurement discussion: universal wave mechanics and correlations between apparatus and observed system]{ref}

Thus collapse is not problematic merely because conditional probabilities change when a record is learned. The problem is the status of that record and of the dynamical rule used to describe it. A defensible interpretation must explain when its updating rule is applicable, what makes a record definite in its own ontology, and why the ordinary measurement statistics follow. No appeal to human consciousness is required by the displayed unitary measurement model.

=== 3
{scope}

==== Solution

The <Bell theorem> gives a powerful conditional constraint, but does not by itself establish the unrestricted philosophical sentence that every interpretation contains a literal instantaneous action between distant objects. One must specify what locality means and which further assumptions are retained. In particular, <Bell local causality> is stronger than an inability to send a faster-than-light message.

Let $a,b$ be settings in spacelike-separated laboratories, $A,B\in\{-1,1\}$ their recorded outcomes, and $\lambda$ a sufficient specification of relevant prior physical variables. A Bell-local model factorizes as
$$
p(A,B\mid a,b,\lambda)=p(A\mid a,\lambda)p(B\mid b,\lambda).
$$
Together with <measurement independence>, $\rho(\lambda\mid a,b)=\rho(\lambda)$, this constrains observable correlations. It allows stochastic response functions: predetermination of all outcomes is not a necessary separate assumption in this general formulation. Bell's 1964 paper demonstrates the incompatibility between a local completion and selected quantum correlations; the two-setting inequality below is a standard later form of the argument, not a claim about the exact notation of that paper.<On the Einstein Podolsky Rosen paradox by John Stewart Bell>[1964, sections II–V; distinguish the original perfect-correlation argument from this stochastic two-setting formulation]{ref}

For example, write the local conditional mean outcomes as $A(a,\lambda),B(b,\lambda)\in[-1,1]$. At each $\lambda$,
$$
|A(a)[B(b)+B(b')]+A(a')[B(b)-B(b')]|\leq2.
$$
Averaging over the same setting-independent distribution gives the <CHSH inequality>, $|S|\leq2$. A suitable <spin singlet state> instead predicts $|S|=2\sqrt2$. Consequently no model retaining the stated factorization and independence assumptions can reproduce all those quantum correlations. Merely replacing deterministic hidden variables with local random noise cannot evade the bound.

However, violation of this bound does not entail operational signalling. For an ordinary entangled state, a remote nonselective measurement leaves the local <reduced density matrix> unchanged. The local experimenter cannot choose the outcome needed to encode a controllable message. <Quantum no-signalling> and failure of Bell factorization can therefore coexist; confusing them would misstate both the prediction and the theorem.

Interpretations allocate the explanatory burden differently. In <Bohmian mechanics>, the guiding dynamics of an entangled configuration is explicitly nonlocal, though quantum equilibrium masks controllable signalling. A collapse interpretation must explain the status of correlations and of any proposed nonlocal state change. Everettian accounts retain the entangled global state and multiple relative records; their defenders can deny a fundamental action that selects one distant outcome. That does not produce a factorizing classical common-cause model of the observed correlations. Whether such an account deserves the word local depends on whether locality means local dynamical interactions, separability, or Bell's single-outcome causal factorization.

One can also reject setting independence or change the assumed causal organization, but such proposals must be developed as physical models, not advertised as refutations of the inequality's proof. The warranted conclusion is that the conjunction of Bell locality, independent settings and the relevant outcome framework is incompatible with the quantum correlations. Calling the failure action-at-a-distance is justified only after an interpretation specifies the physical entities, events and causal commitments to which that phrase applies.

=== 4
{scope}

==== Solution

In the specific <Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen completeness criterion>, completeness is not just an ability to predict all experimental probabilities. A complete theory must represent every element of physical reality. The associated sufficient reality criterion says that a quantity has such an element if its value can be predicted with certainty without disturbing the system. The EPR paper distinguishes these demands from correctness of the theory's empirical predictions.<Can Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality Be Considered Complete? by Albert Einstein and others>[1935, page 777, statement of the reality and completeness criteria; pages 779–780, separated-system argument]{ref}

Their position–momentum example makes the locality assumption crucial. For two systems with ideal correlations,
$$
X_A-X_B=d,\qquad P_A+P_B=0,
$$
the two joint observables commute, although $[X_B,P_B]=i\hbar$. Measuring $X_A$ permits a certain inference of $X_B$; choosing instead to measure $P_A$ permits a certain inference of $P_B$. If a spacelike-separated choice at A cannot alter the physical reality at B, the choice should not determine which properties B possesses. Combining that independence with the reality criterion motivates assigning both quantities to B, while an ordinary individual wave function does not supply both sharp values.

This is not a procedure for experimentally measuring both noncommuting quantities on B at once. It is a counterfactual argument comparing alternative remote measurements, supported by a locality claim about the same remote system. The ideal perfectly correlated position–momentum state is also nonnormalizable; normalizable approximations approach its correlations. The philosophical inference is best assessed in the ideal limit with this qualification stated.

<Niels Bohr>'s reply challenges the criterion's application. He distinguishes a direct mechanical disturbance of the remote system from a dependence of the conditions under which a definite prediction can be meaningfully specified. His appeal to <quantum complementarity> is therefore not simply the claim that the particles mechanically push one another during the final measurement. It disputes the context-independent assignment of the two elements of reality.<Can Quantum-Mechanical Description of Physical Reality Be Considered Complete? by Niels Bohr>[1935, pages 699–700, discussion of alternative arrangements and the ambiguity in the reality criterion]{ref} Whether that response answers EPR's locality challenge is itself a substantive philosophical issue; it is not resolved by restating the uncertainty relation.

A qualified assessment is preferable to an unlabelled yes or no. If EPR's locality and reality premises are retained, the wave-function description is incomplete in their sense. But the later <Bell theorem> shows that adding ordinary local hidden variables cannot simply recover all quantum correlations while keeping the standard independence assumptions. The natural completion EPR hoped for cannot retain that entire package.

Nonlocal <Bohmian mechanics> provides one kind of completion with definite configurations. Everettian wave-only accounts use another notion of completeness while rejecting the premise of one absolute set of distant outcomes. An instrumental reading can regard the quantum formalism as complete for assigning experimental probabilities without accepting EPR's representational demand at all. Thus predictive completeness, completeness of an interpretation's ontology, and completeness in EPR's particular sense are different judgements. The EPR argument exposes a conflict among commitments; it does not establish a criterion-independent verdict about every interpretation.

=== 5
{scope}

==== Solution

<Born rule> probabilities can be given a coherent role in an Everettian interpretation, but that role cannot simply be the chance that one branch alone will be created. In the <many-worlds interpretation>, unitary measurement produces all of the decohering outcome components. The question is why their squared amplitudes should govern rational expectation, typical recorded frequencies or concern for future observers.

For a state $\sum_i c_i|s_i\rangle$ and a measuring apparatus, the resulting correlated state has components $c_i|s_i\rangle|A_i\rangle$. The squared norms $w_i=|c_i|^2$ supply additive <branch weights>. They are preserved by unitary refinements of an outcome into orthogonal subcomponents: if a branch splits internally, its descendants' weights sum to its original weight. Everett's original paper introduces the relative-state description and a measure associated with the statistical analysis; this should not be confused with an independent stochastic collapse law.<Relative State Formulation of Quantum Mechanics by Hugh Everett III>[1957, statistical interpretation and relative-state measurement discussion; the modern probability question concerns the interpretation of the measure, not just its normalization]{ref}

A simple example shows why counting branches is not an alternative rule without further structure. Begin with
$$
|\psi\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt3}|A\rangle+\sqrt{\frac23}|B\rangle.
$$
If an irrelevant environment interaction refines B into two orthogonal components of equal amplitude, there are now three displayed components, each of amplitude $1/\sqrt3$. The total weight of B is still $2/3$. Counting displayed components instead changes A's assigned share from $1/2$ to $1/3$, merely because the description has resolved additional environmental detail. Real decoherent branching is approximate and has no unique fundamental branch count. Squared norm, in contrast, respects such refinements.

There are at least two ways to understand the remaining epistemic issue. An observer may have <self-locating uncertainty in Everettian quantum mechanics>, especially after branching but before accessing a record: the uncertainty concerns which centred observer situation is theirs, not ignorance of the global wave function. Alternatively, a decision-theoretic account can ask how a present observer should value all future successors without assuming that exactly one successor is uniquely them. These are distinct proposals, and the second does not become incoherent simply because the first is disputed.

<David Wallace>'s decision-theoretic programme argues that suitable rationality and measurement-equivalence assumptions lead to expected-utility weights equal to squared amplitudes. The assumptions, including the appropriate neutrality of the measurement implementation, do real work.<Quantum Probability and Decision Theory, Revisited by David Wallace>[2002 preprint, sections 4–5 on equivalence, measurement neutrality and decision-theoretic arguments]{ref} A critic can question whether the constraints already privilege the desired measure or whether a preference theorem establishes the empirical role of chance. The defender must answer those objections; unitarity and normalization alone do not automatically turn a deterministic global theory into ordinary single-outcome randomness.

Repeated experiments add a consistency check. With Born weights, the total weight of frequency records is binomial and concentrates near the Born frequency as the number of trials increases. Yet calling the high-weight records typical already uses a measure, so this is not by itself a noncircular derivation of the weighting rule. The balanced conclusion is that Born probabilities are not meaningless in Everettian quantum mechanics: they can organize credence, decisions and typical records. Their status requires an argued connection between branch weights and those roles, rather than a bare appeal either to all outcomes happening or to the algebraic identity $\sum_i|c_i|^2=1$.

== S30
{c}
{scope}

=== 1
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

Use the relative star–planet separation and specific <angular momentum> per unit reduced mass,
$$
\boxed{J=r^2\dot\theta.}
$$
For $r=l/(1+e\cos\theta)$, differentiation gives $\dot r=Je\sin\theta/l$, while the transverse speed is $r\dot\theta=J/r=J(1+e\cos\theta)/l$. Adding the squared orthogonal components yields
$$
\boxed{v^2=\frac{J^2}{l^2}(1+2e\cos\theta+e^2).}
$$
The <Binet equation> for the relative two-body <inverse-square force> is $u''+u=G(M+m)/J^2$, with $u=1/r$. The conic has $u''+u=1/l$, so
$$
\boxed{J^2=G(M+m)l.}
$$
The printed division by $l$ is an error: $J^2$ has dimensions $\mathrm{length^4/time^2}$, while $G(M+m)/l$ has dimensions of speed squared. A circular orbit independently checks the correction: $J^2=r^2v^2=G(M+m)r$, with $l=r$.

For a closed ellipse, $l=a(1-e^2)$ and $1+2e\cos\theta+e^2=2l/r-(1-e^2)$. Substitution now gives the <vis-viva equation>,
$$
\boxed{v^2=G(M+m)\left(\frac2r-\frac1a\right).}
$$
This is relative orbital speed; either body's speed about the barycentre follows by the corresponding mass-ratio factor.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

A circular orbit has uniform angular motion, so its <Keplerian radial-velocity curve> is sinusoidal. Choose phase $\phi=(t-t_{\rm tr})/P$, positive stellar velocity away from the observer, and an edge-on circular orbit. One consistent convention is
$$
\boxed{V-V_0=-K\sin(2\pi\phi).}
$$
Transit occurs at the decreasing systemic-velocity crossing $\phi=0$; secondary eclipse is at the increasing crossing $\phi=1/2$. Extrema occur one quarter of an orbit away. Reversing the velocity sign convention reverses the crossing directions but not the conjunction phases.

For an eccentric orbit, $\nu$ is not proportional to time. Solve $\mathcal M=E-e\sin E$, then use $\tan(\nu/2)=\sqrt{(1+e)/(1-e)}\tan(E/2)$ in the stated velocity relation. Motion is fastest near periastron, so the curve has an abrupt feature there and a more prolonged change near apastron, with its shape also depending on $\varpi$. It is not merely a shifted sinusoid. An eccentric transit need not coincide with $V=V_0$, because the $e\cos\varpi$ offset remains at conjunction.

\Image[2015-s30-radial-velocity-and-atmospheric-annulus.png]
{title=Circular and eccentric stellar radial velocities, and a transmission-spectrum annulus}
{description=Original calculated velocity curves use a circular transit as phase zero on the left, and periastron as phase zero for the separate eccentric example with $e=0.5$ and $\varpi=0$. The right panel illustrates the projected area added by one atmospheric scale height; its atmospheric thickness is exaggerated for visibility.}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

Assume the companion is much less massive than the star, the sinusoid represents a circular Keplerian orbit, and the transit implies $\sin i\simeq1$. Ignore dilution, limb darkening and grazing geometry when estimating the radius. With $P=5\times86400\,\mathrm s$,
$$
a\simeq\left(\frac{GM_\odot P^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^{1/3}
=8.56\times10^9\,\mathrm m=0.0572\,\mathrm{AU}.
$$
The stellar reflex <radial-velocity semi-amplitude> gives $K\simeq(m/M_\odot)(2\pi a/P)$, while transit depth gives $\delta\simeq(R_p/R_\odot)^2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{m\simeq1.12\times10^{27}\,\mathrm{kg}=0.589M_{\rm Jup},\qquad
R_p\simeq9.84\times10^7\,\mathrm m=1.408R_{\rm Jup}.}
$$
The <mean density> relative to the supplied Jupiter values is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\bar\rho_p}{\bar\rho_{\rm Jup}}\simeq\frac{0.589}{1.408^3}=0.211,\qquad
\bar\rho_p\simeq2.80\times10^2\,\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}}.}
$$
This is a low-density hot gas giant. Strong irradiation can slow contraction or help sustain an inflated envelope; internal heating and a different heavy-element fraction are other possibilities. The inferred low mass also reduces gravitational compression. Mass and radius alone do not determine which inflation mechanism operates.

=== 2
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

The source expression $E=\tfrac12\mu v^2-GMm/r$ is **total mechanical energy** in the centre-of-mass frame, not kinetic energy alone. Take $v_0$ as the relative speed at a large initial separation where the mutual potential is negligible. At closest approach, the radial relative velocity is zero, so <conservation of angular momentum> and <conservation of energy> give
$$
\mu b v_0=\mu r_cv_c,\qquad
\frac12\mu v_0^2=\frac12\mu v_c^2-\frac{GMm}{r_c}.
$$
Using $Mm/\mu=M+m$ eliminates $v_c$:
$$
b^2=r_c^2+\frac{2G(M+m)r_c}{v_0^2}.
$$
An impact occurs when $r_c\leq R$, where $R=r_M+r_m$. Since the right side increases with positive $r_c$, the collision threshold is
$$
\boxed{b_{\rm col}^2=R^2\left[1+\frac{2G(M+m)}{Rv_0^2}\right],\qquad
f=\frac{2G(M+m)}{Rv_0^2}.}
$$
The mutual <escape velocity> is the relative speed at contact required to reach infinite separation with zero residual speed. Setting total mechanical energy to zero gives
$$
\boxed{v_{\rm esc}=\sqrt{\frac{2G(M+m)}{r_M+r_m}},\qquad f=v_{\rm esc}^2/v_0^2.}
$$
This includes target recoil. If the given initial speed were instead at a finite non-negligible separation, its potential energy would have to be retained; $v_0$ would then not equal the asymptotic speed used in the focusing formula.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The collision <cross-section> in the incoming impact-parameter plane is
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{\rm col}=\pi b_{\rm col}^2
=\pi(r_M+r_m)^2(1+f).}
$$
Without <gravity> it is only $\sigma_{\rm geom}=\pi(r_M+r_m)^2$, so <gravitational focusing> increases the cross-section by $1+f$. Trajectories that would miss geometrically can be bent into contact, especially for slow relative encounters. Gravity does not itself guarantee sticking: the following accretion estimate assumes collisions agglomerate rather than fragment, rebound or erode the target.

A uniform incoming projectile mass density $\rho_p$ has mass flux $\rho_pv_0$, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{dM}{dt}=\rho_pv_0\sigma_{\rm col}.}
$$
The formula counts mass swept per unit time and assumes an adequate continuing projectile supply, the stated speed, and negligible many-body or stellar-tidal modification of the two-body cross-section.

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

In the strong-focusing, small-projectile limit, write $R_M=(3M/(4\pi\rho_s))^{1/3}$ with constant solid-material density. Then
$$
\sigma_{\rm col}\simeq\frac{2\pi GMR_M}{v_0^2},\qquad
\dot M=A M^{4/3},\qquad
A=\frac{2\pi G\rho_p}{v_0}\left(\frac3{4\pi\rho_s}\right)^{1/3}.
$$
Integrating $dM/M^{4/3}=A\,dt$ from $M_0$ to $M$ gives
$$
t=\frac3A(M_0^{-1/3}-M^{-1/3})
\simeq\boxed{3M_0^{-1/3}\left(\frac{4\pi\rho_s}{3}\right)^{1/3}\frac{v_0}{2\pi G\rho_p}}
$$
for $M\gg M_0$. The fractional growth rate $\dot M/M\propto M^{1/3}$ increases with mass: larger bodies gain an increasing advantage, hence <gravitationally focused growth of a planetary core> is called runaway accretion in this regime.

The formal finite time to unlimited mass is not physical. Growth depletes the feeding region; embryos also stir planetesimal velocities and reduce focusing, while fragmentation, orbital competition and stellar tides alter the encounter geometry. A growing core can enter an oligarchic rather than runaway regime, and eventually reach a supply-limited isolation mass.

<Dynamical friction> transfers random kinetic energy from a massive body to a swarm and tends toward $Mv_M^2\sim mv_m^2$. Larger bodies therefore have smaller random eccentricities and inclinations, remain concentrated near the planetesimal layer, and can retain favorable encounter conditions. When their own random motion contributes appreciably to relative speed, damping it also strengthens focusing through $f\propto v_0^{-2}$. If the small-body dispersion already dominates $v_0$, equipartition of the core alone does not further lower that encounter speed: the displayed growth law assumed a fixed $v_0$, not an automatically imposed $v_0\propto M^{-1/2}$.

=== 3
{scope}

==== 1
{scope}

===== Solution

During a <exoplanet transit>, starlight traverses the planetary limb. The effective opaque radius varies with wavelength because the altitude of slant optical depth near unity depends on molecular absorption and scattering. Differencing the in-transit and out-of-transit stellar spectra measures this wavelength-dependent blocked area, the <exoplanet transmission spectrum>. Stellar spots and faculae can contaminate that difference and require control.

During a <exoplanet secondary eclipse>, the disappearance of planetary light permits subtraction of the stellar contribution. Visible planetary light is predominantly reflected starlight, sensitive to clouds, hazes, <Rayleigh scattering>, and atomic sodium and potassium absorption. Infrared planetary emission and limb absorption show rotational-vibrational features of species such as water, carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide and methane, together with collision-induced absorption involving molecular hydrogen. Thermal emission also depends on the atmospheric temperature-pressure profile; a temperature inversion can turn a molecular absorption feature into emission. Which species are prominent depends on composition, temperature and the observed band, rather than being universal to every planet.

==== 2
{scope}

===== Solution

The measured mass and radius imply
$$
\bar\rho_p=\rho_\oplus\frac{6.6}{2.6^3}\simeq2.1\,\mathrm{g\,cm^{-3}}.
$$
Compared with the supplied composition curves, this is inconsistent with a bare iron-rich or ordinary rock-only planet. A volatile-rich interior and/or an extended low-density gas envelope is needed. A water-rich body with an atmosphere and a rocky core with a modest H/He envelope are different plausible families; mass and radius do not uniquely determine the composition.<Mass-radius relationships for solid exoplanets by Sara Seager and others>[2007, abstract and solid-composition mass-radius models; the observed radius can also include a gas envelope]{ref}

For the requested H/He estimate, assume a hydrostatic, approximately isothermal terminator at the zero-Bond-albedo, fully redistributed equilibrium temperature. The orbit and temperature are
$$
a=\left[\frac{G(0.16M_\odot)P^2}{4\pi^2}\right]^{1/3}=0.0144\,\mathrm{AU},\qquad
T_{\rm eq}=3000\sqrt{\frac{0.21R_\odot}{2a}}\simeq552\,\mathrm K.
$$
Surface gravity is $g=Gm/R_p^2\simeq9.58\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. With molar mass $\mathcal M=2.2\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{kg\,mol^{-1}}$, the <atmospheric scale height> is
$$
\boxed{H=\frac{R_uT}{\mathcal M g}\simeq2.18\times10^5\,\mathrm m\simeq218\,\mathrm{km}.}
$$
A spectral feature spanning $N$ scale heights changes the blocked stellar fraction by the atmospheric annulus area:
$$
\boxed{\Delta\delta\simeq\frac{2R_pNH}{R_*^2}\simeq3.38\times10^{-4}N.}
$$
Thus one scale height is about $338$ parts per million in transit depth; a clear strong feature spanning roughly five scale heights is of order $1.7\times10^{-3}$. The number of scale heights requires the actual opacity ratio; $N\simeq\ln(\kappa_{\rm line}/\kappa_{\rm cont})$ is a useful isothermal estimate, not a supplied measured value. The true terminator temperature need not equal this redistribution estimate.

A nearly flat spectrum can result from a high-mean-molecular-mass atmosphere with a much smaller scale height, or from optically thick clouds/hazes that hide the deeper absorption features. These are the two qualitative possibilities; detailed precision data can discriminate them. In particular, the pre-2015 HST study favored clouds over the clear high-molecular-mass models it tested, so listing both physical mechanisms is not a claim that all such models fit those data equally well.https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0022[2014 transmission-spectrum study, abstract: featureless near-infrared spectrum and rejection of tested cloud-free high-molecular-mass models]{ref}

==== 3
{scope}

===== Solution

The <circumstellar habitable zone> is the range of stellar irradiation at which a suitable rocky planet could maintain liquid water at its surface under specified atmospheric conditions. It is not a guarantee of life or even of liquid water on every planet at that distance. Its boundaries depend on greenhouse gases, clouds, humidity, pressure, albedo, rotation and the stellar spectrum; stellar evolution, ultraviolet activity, atmospheric loss and tidal locking can complicate applications to cool stars.

Approximating both stars as <blackbodies>, the host luminosity relative to the Sun is
$$
\frac{L_*}{L_\odot}=0.21^2\left(\frac{3000}{5778}\right)^4\simeq0.00320.
$$
The Earth-irradiance orbit therefore has
$$
\boxed{a_{\rm E}=\sqrt{L_*/L_\odot}\,\mathrm{AU}\simeq0.0566\,\mathrm{AU},\qquad
\theta_{\max}\simeq\frac{a_{\rm E}}{14.6\,\mathrm{pc}}=3.88\,\mathrm{mas}.}
$$
Use the largest projected separation and a visible reference wavelength of $550\,\mathrm{nm}$. The <Rayleigh criterion> for a circular aperture gives
$$
\boxed{D\gtrsim\frac{1.22\lambda}{\theta_{\max}}\simeq35.7\left(\frac\lambda{550\,\mathrm{nm}}\right)\mathrm m.}
$$
This is only a diffraction-based minimum: detecting a faint planet beside its star additionally requires contrast suppression and adequate photon counts. An interferometer baseline, rather than its collecting-aperture diameter, sets its angular resolution.

For reflected light the <reflected-light planetary phase curve> gives the flux ratio $\epsilon=A_g\Phi(\alpha)(R_\oplus/a_{\rm E})^2$, where $A_g$ is <geometric albedo> and $\Phi(0)=1$. Numerically,
$$
\boxed{\epsilon_{\rm full}\simeq5.66\times10^{-7}A_g.}
$$
A unit-geometric-albedo reference gives about $6\times10^{-7}$; an Earth-like chosen $A_g=0.3$ gives $1.7\times10^{-7}$. Albedo and phase function were not specified, so an exact contrast cannot be inferred just from the radius. Full-phase brightness occurs close to zero projected separation for an edge-on orbit; at the best-resolved quadrature, a Lambert sphere has $\Phi(\pi/2)=1/\pi$, reducing the Earth-albedo example to $5.4\times10^{-8}$. Thus the best angular separation and maximum reflected contrast are not generally achieved at the same phase.
